// --- Lesson 1: The Roots of Communism and the 1917 Revolutions ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch1_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## What is Communism? Karl Marx and the Origins of Revolutionary Thought\n\nCommunism is a political and economic ideology that advocates for a classless society in which the means of production (factories, farms, mines) are owned collectively rather than by private individuals. The theory was developed primarily by **Karl Marx** (1818-1883) and **Friedrich Engels** (1820-1895).\n\n### Key Ideas of Marxism\n\n1. **Historical materialism**: History is driven by economic forces and class struggle, not by great individuals or ideas\n2. **Class struggle**: Society is divided into the **bourgeoisie** (owners of the means of production) and the **proletariat** (workers who sell their labour)\n3. **Surplus value**: Capitalists exploit workers by paying them less than the value of what they produce\n4. **Dialectical materialism**: Social change happens through conflict between opposing forces (thesis vs antithesis = synthesis)\n5. **Dictatorship of the proletariat**: After revolution, workers would temporarily control the state before it withered away\n6. **Communist utopia**: Eventually the state would disappear and a classless, stateless society would emerge\n\nMarx and Engels published **The Communist Manifesto** in 1848, famously declaring: *"Workers of the world, unite! You have nothing to lose but your chains."*'),
  q(2, 'According to Karl Marx, what is the driving force of history?',
    ['Economic forces and class struggle', 'Great leaders and their decisions', 'Religious beliefs and moral values', 'Technological inventions alone'], 0,
    'Marx argued that history is shaped by economic relations and the struggle between classes. He called this historical materialism, where the economic base of society determines its political and cultural superstructure.'),
  t(3, '## Russia Before the Revolution\n\nBy 1900, Russia was an autocracy ruled by **Tsar Nicholas II**. It was one of the most backward countries in Europe:\n\n| Feature | Russia in 1900 |\n|---------|----------------|\n| **Government** | Absolute monarchy; no parliament, no constitution |\n| **Economy** | 80% peasant farmers; beginning to industrialise |\n| **Social structure** | Nobility, small middle class, vast peasantry, growing urban working class |\n| **Literacy** | Only about 25% of the population could read |\n| **Opposition** | Illegal; secret police (Okhrana) suppressed dissent |\n\n### Political Opposition Groups\n\n- **Social Revolutionaries (SRs)**: Focused on the peasantry; believed in land redistribution; used terrorism\n- **Social Democrats**: Marxists who believed the urban working class would lead the revolution. In 1903 they split into:\n  - **Bolsheviks** (meaning "majority"): Led by **Vladimir Lenin**; favoured a small, disciplined party of professional revolutionaries\n  - **Mensheviks** (meaning "minority"): Favoured a broad-based, democratic party open to all workers\n- **Liberals (Kadets)**: Middle-class professionals who wanted a constitutional monarchy and parliament'),
  fb(4, 'In 1903, the Russian Social Democrats split into two factions: the ___, led by Lenin, and the Mensheviks. The Bolsheviks favoured a small, disciplined party of professional ___.',
    ['Bolsheviks', 'revolutionaries'],
    'The Bolsheviks (meaning "majority") were led by Lenin and advocated a tightly organised vanguard party of professional revolutionaries, not a mass party open to all workers.'),
  t(5, '## The February Revolution of 1917\n\nThe **February Revolution** (March 1917 by the Western calendar) was a spontaneous uprising that ended over 300 years of Romanov rule.\n\n### Causes\n\n1. **World War I disasters**: Russia suffered catastrophic defeats; over 2 million soldiers killed by 1917; severe food and ammunition shortages\n2. **Economic crisis**: War caused inflation, food shortages, and transport breakdown; bread queues in Petrograd (St Petersburg)\n3. **Rasputin**: The mystic Grigori Rasputin had enormous influence over Tsarina Alexandra, discrediting the monarchy; he was murdered in December 1916\n4. **Weak leadership**: Tsar Nicholas II took personal command of the army in 1915, leaving the incompetent Tsarina in charge of government\n5. **Growing unrest**: Strikes, protests, and mutinies became widespread\n\n### The Revolution\n\n- **23 February 1917**: Women workers in Petrograd marched demanding bread (International Women\'s Day)\n- Protests grew; workers joined; soldiers refused to fire on crowds and joined the protesters\n- **2 March 1917**: Tsar Nicholas II **abdicated** (gave up the throne)\n- A **Provisional Government** was formed, led first by Prince Lvov, then by Alexander Kerensky\n- Alongside it, workers and soldiers formed **soviets** (councils) creating **dual power**'),
  q(6, 'What was the immediate trigger for the February Revolution of 1917?',
    ['Women workers in Petrograd marching for bread on International Women\'s Day', 'Lenin returning from exile in Switzerland', 'The assassination of Rasputin', 'A military coup by army generals'], 0,
    'On 23 February 1917 (International Women\'s Day), women textile workers in Petrograd went on strike and marched demanding bread. The protests spread rapidly, and when soldiers refused to fire on the crowds, the regime collapsed within days.'),
  t(7, '## The October Revolution of 1917\n\nBetween February and October 1917, the Provisional Government proved weak and unpopular:\n\n- It continued fighting in World War I (deeply unpopular)\n- It failed to redistribute land to peasants\n- It failed to address food shortages\n- It shared power uneasily with the Petrograd Soviet\n\n### Lenin Returns\n\n- In April 1917, the Germans helped Lenin return to Russia from exile in Switzerland (in a sealed train)\n- Lenin immediately issued the **April Theses**: "Peace, Land, Bread" and "All Power to the Soviets"\n- He argued there should be no cooperation with the Provisional Government\n\n### The Bolshevik Seizure of Power\n\n- By autumn 1917, the Bolsheviks had won majorities in key soviets, including Petrograd and Moscow\n- On **25 October 1917** (7 November Western calendar), the Bolsheviks, organised by **Leon Trotsky**, seized key buildings in Petrograd\n- The cruiser **Aurora** fired a blank shot signalling the storming of the **Winter Palace**\n- The Provisional Government was arrested; Kerensky fled\n- Lenin declared: *"We shall now proceed to construct the socialist order"*\n- This was NOT a popular mass uprising but a well-planned **coup** by a disciplined minority'),
  q(8, 'What was Lenin\'s key slogan when he returned to Russia in April 1917?',
    ['Peace, Land, Bread', 'Workers of the world, unite', 'Liberty, Equality, Fraternity', 'All power to the Tsar'], 0,
    'Lenin\'s April Theses called for "Peace, Land, Bread" and "All Power to the Soviets." This was a brilliant piece of propaganda that addressed the three things Russians wanted most: an end to the war, land for the peasants, and food.'),
  fb(9, 'The October Revolution was organised militarily by ___ and took place on 25 October 1917. The Bolsheviks stormed the ___ Palace to arrest the Provisional Government.',
    ['Trotsky', 'Winter'],
    'Leon Trotsky, as chairman of the Petrograd Soviet\'s Military Revolutionary Committee, organised the practical details of the Bolshevik seizure of power. The Winter Palace was the seat of the Provisional Government.'),
  t(10, '## Lenin Seizes Control: Consolidating Power (1917-1921)\n\nAfter seizing power, Lenin moved quickly to consolidate Bolshevik control:\n\n### Immediate Decrees\n\n1. **Decree on Peace**: Called for an immediate end to the war\n2. **Decree on Land**: Abolished private land ownership; land was redistributed to peasants\n3. **Decree on Workers\' Control**: Workers\' committees could oversee factory management\n4. **Treaty of Brest-Litovsk (March 1918)**: Russia withdrew from WWI, surrendering vast territories (Ukraine, Finland, Baltic states, Poland) to Germany\n\n### The Constituent Assembly\n\n- In November 1917 elections, the **Social Revolutionaries** won the most seats (370 vs Bolsheviks\' 175)\n- Lenin allowed the Assembly to meet on **5 January 1918** but dissolved it after just one day\n- This ended any hope of democracy in Russia\n\n### Establishing a One-Party State\n\n- Other political parties were banned\n- The **Cheka** (secret police) was created in December 1917 under Felix Dzerzhinsky to crush opposition\n- Press censorship was introduced; opposition newspapers shut down\n- The Tsar and his family were **executed** at Yekaterinburg in **July 1918**'),
  q(11, 'What happened when the Constituent Assembly met in January 1918?',
    ['Lenin dissolved it after one day because the Bolsheviks did not have a majority', 'It voted to support Lenin\'s government unanimously', 'It established a democratic constitution for Russia', 'It was dissolved by the Mensheviks'], 0,
    'The Bolsheviks lost the November 1917 election to the SRs. When the Constituent Assembly met on 5 January 1918, Lenin used Bolshevik soldiers to shut it down after a single day. This showed that Lenin was committed to Bolshevik power, not democracy.'),
  t(12, '### The Russian Civil War (1918-1921)\n\nA devastating civil war broke out between:\n- **Reds** (Bolsheviks): Led by Trotsky, who created the **Red Army** from scratch and enforced discipline with political commissars\n- **Whites** (anti-Bolsheviks): A loose coalition of monarchists, liberals, SRs, Mensheviks, and foreign interventionists\n- Foreign powers (Britain, France, USA, Japan) sent troops and supplies to support the Whites\n\n**Why the Reds won:**\n1. Trotsky\u2019s brilliant military leadership and the use of political commissars\n2. The Whites were divided \u2014 they had no common aim except defeating the Bolsheviks\n3. **War Communism**: The state took control of all industry, requisitioned grain from peasants, and banned private trade to feed the Red Army\n4. The Bolsheviks controlled the centre of Russia (Petrograd, Moscow, railway network)\n5. The Cheka used **Red Terror** \u2014 mass executions, labour camps, and intimidation\n\nThe civil war caused enormous suffering: an estimated **7-10 million people died** from fighting, famine, and disease. War Communism caused a massive famine in 1921-1922.'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: NEP, Marxism-Leninism, and the Death of Lenin ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch1_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## The New Economic Policy (NEP) 1921-1928\n\nBy 1921, War Communism had devastated Russia. Peasants were starving, workers were striking, and in March 1921 the sailors at **Kronstadt** (previously loyal Bolshevik supporters) mutinied demanding free elections and economic reform.\n\nLenin recognised that change was essential. At the Tenth Party Congress (March 1921), he introduced the **New Economic Policy (NEP)**.\n\n### Key Features of the NEP\n\n| Feature | Detail |\n|---------|--------|\n| **Agriculture** | Peasants paid a tax in kind (a percentage of their crop) and could sell the surplus on the open market |\n| **Small businesses** | Private ownership of small businesses (under 20 workers) was allowed |\n| **Large industry** | The state retained control of banks, heavy industry, and foreign trade (the "commanding heights") |\n| **Trade** | Private trade (buying and selling) was legalised again |\n| **Currency** | A new stable currency was introduced |\n\n### Results of the NEP\n\n- Agricultural and industrial production recovered to pre-war levels by 1926\n- A new class of wealthy peasants (**kulaks**) and private traders (**Nepmen**) emerged\n- Many Bolsheviks were uncomfortable \u2014 the NEP seemed like a retreat to capitalism\n- Lenin called it "one step backwards in order to take two steps forward"'),
  q(2, 'Why did Lenin introduce the New Economic Policy (NEP) in 1921?',
    ['War Communism had caused economic collapse, famine, and the Kronstadt mutiny', 'He wanted to permanently return to capitalism', 'Trotsky pressured him to abandon socialism', 'Foreign powers demanded economic reform as a condition for peace'], 0,
    'By 1921, War Communism had caused famine, economic collapse, and unrest. The Kronstadt mutiny (March 1921) showed that even loyal supporters were turning against the Bolsheviks. Lenin introduced the NEP as a temporary strategic retreat.'),
  t(3, '## Marxism-Leninism: How Lenin Adapted Marx\n\nMarx had predicted that communist revolution would occur in **advanced industrial countries** (like Britain or Germany) where a large proletariat existed. Russia was predominantly agricultural with a small working class. Lenin adapted Marxism significantly:\n\n### Key Differences Between Marx and Lenin\n\n| Marx\'s Theory | Lenin\'s Adaptation |\n|---------------|-------------------|\n| Revolution by the proletariat (working class) | Revolution led by a **vanguard party** of professional revolutionaries |\n| Revolution in industrialised countries | Revolution possible in backward, peasant countries |\n| The state would wither away after revolution | The state must be strengthened to defend the revolution |\n| Democratic workers\' councils | Centralised, disciplined party making decisions from above |\n| International revolution | "Socialism in one country" possible (later developed by Stalin) |\n\nThis adapted ideology became known as **Marxism-Leninism** and became the official ideology of the Soviet Union.\n\n### Democratic Centralism\n\nLenin established the principle of **democratic centralism**: ideas could be debated freely within the party, but once a decision was reached, all members must obey it without question. In practice, this meant the leadership dictated policy.'),
  fb(4, 'Lenin argued that revolution would be led by a ___ party of professional revolutionaries, not by a spontaneous mass uprising. The principle that all party members must obey decisions once reached was called democratic ___.',
    ['vanguard', 'centralism'],
    'Lenin adapted Marx by arguing that a small, disciplined vanguard party was needed to lead the workers to revolution. Democratic centralism meant debate before a decision but total obedience afterwards.'),
  t(5, '## The Death of Lenin and the Power Struggle\n\nLenin suffered a series of strokes from May 1922 and died on **21 January 1924**. Before his death, he wrote a political testament warning about the leading party members:\n\n- He criticised **Stalin** as too rude and recommended his removal as General Secretary\n- He praised **Trotsky** as the most capable but warned he was too arrogant\n- He warned against the party splitting into factions\n\n**Lenin\u2019s testament was suppressed** by the Central Committee and never made public during the power struggle.\n\n### The Main Contenders\n\n| Contender | Position | Key Ideas |\n|-----------|---------|----------|\n| **Leon Trotsky** | Commissar of War | Permanent revolution (spread communism worldwide); end the NEP rapidly |\n| **Joseph Stalin** | General Secretary | Socialism in one country (build communism in USSR first); pragmatic |\n| **Grigory Zinoviev** | Leningrad party boss | Initially allied with Stalin against Trotsky |\n| **Lev Kamenev** | Moscow party boss | Initially allied with Stalin against Trotsky |\n| **Nikolai Bukharin** | Editor of Pravda | Right wing; supported continuing the NEP |'),
  q(6, 'What did Lenin recommend in his political testament regarding Stalin?',
    ['That Stalin should be removed as General Secretary because he was too rude', 'That Stalin should become the next leader of the USSR', 'That Stalin and Trotsky should share power equally', 'That Stalin should focus on economic reform'], 0,
    'Lenin\'s testament warned that Stalin had concentrated too much power in his hands as General Secretary and was "too rude." Lenin recommended his removal. However, the Central Committee suppressed the testament and Stalin used his position to outmanoeuvre his rivals.'),
  t(7, '### How Stalin Won the Power Struggle\n\nStalin was underestimated by his rivals. As **General Secretary**, he controlled party appointments, placing loyal supporters in key positions throughout the country.\n\n**Stage 1 (1924-1925):** Stalin allied with Zinoviev and Kamenev against Trotsky\n- They accused Trotsky of trying to split the party and of being disloyal to Lenin\n- Trotsky was removed as Commissar of War (1925)\n\n**Stage 2 (1925-1927):** Stalin allied with Bukharin against Zinoviev and Kamenev\n- Zinoviev and Kamenev (now calling themselves the "United Opposition" with Trotsky) were expelled from the party (1927)\n- Trotsky was exiled to Central Asia (1928) and expelled from the USSR (1929)\n\n**Stage 3 (1928-1929):** Stalin turned against Bukharin\n- Stalin now abandoned the NEP (which Bukharin supported) in favour of rapid industrialisation\n- Bukharin was removed from all positions of influence\n\nBy **1929**, Stalin had become the **undisputed leader** of the USSR. He had outmanoeuvred every rival through political cunning, not ideological brilliance.'),
  q(8, 'How did Stalin use his position as General Secretary to win the power struggle?',
    ['He controlled party appointments, placing his supporters in key positions throughout the country', 'He used military force to arrest his opponents', 'He was the most popular leader among ordinary Russian workers', 'He had the support of Lenin\'s political testament'], 0,
    'As General Secretary, Stalin controlled who was appointed to party positions across the USSR. This meant he could build a base of loyal supporters who would vote for him at party congresses. His rivals dismissed this role as mere bureaucratic work, which proved to be a fatal underestimation.'),
  fb(9, 'Trotsky advocated for ___ revolution (spreading communism worldwide), while Stalin promoted socialism in one ___.',
    ['permanent', 'country'],
    'Trotsky believed the revolution could only survive if it spread to other countries (permanent revolution). Stalin argued that communism could be built successfully in the USSR alone (socialism in one country). This debate was central to the power struggle.'),
  t(10, '## South African Context: Communism and the CPSA\n\nThe influence of communism was not limited to Russia. In South Africa, the **Communist Party of South Africa (CPSA)** was founded in **1921**, making it one of the oldest communist parties on the African continent.\n\n- Initially focused on white workers, the CPSA gradually shifted to include black workers and anti-racist struggle\n- The CPSA supported black trade unions and the ANC\u2019s campaigns against racial discrimination\n- In 1950, the apartheid government passed the **Suppression of Communism Act**, which banned the CPSA and defined "communism" so broadly that any opposition to apartheid could be labelled as communist\n- The party reconstituted itself underground as the **South African Communist Party (SACP)** in 1953\n- Many ANC leaders (including Nelson Mandela) were accused of being communists by the apartheid government\n\nThis South African connection shows how ideas of communism travelled globally and were adapted to local conditions, much as Lenin had adapted Marx\u2019s ideas to Russian conditions.'),
];

// --- Lesson 3: Stalin - Collectivisation, Industrialisation, and Purges ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch1_lesson3 = [
  t(1, '## Stalin\'s Economic Revolution: Collectivisation\n\nOnce Stalin consolidated power, he ended the NEP and launched a massive programme to transform the Soviet Union from a backward agricultural country into an industrial superpower.\n\n### Collectivisation of Agriculture (from 1929)\n\nStalin forced peasants to give up their private farms and join **collective farms (kolkhozy)**:\n\n| Feature | Detail |\n|---------|--------|\n| **Aim** | Modernise agriculture; feed the growing urban workforce; export grain to fund industrialisation |\n| **Method** | Peasant land, livestock, and tools were pooled into large state-controlled farms |\n| **Targets** | Kulaks (wealthy peasants) were declared "class enemies" and targeted for elimination as a class |\n\n### Dekulakisation\n\n- An estimated **5-7 million kulaks** were deported to labour camps (the Gulag), exiled to Siberia, or executed\n- Many peasants resisted by slaughtering their livestock rather than handing it over \u2014 by 1933, livestock numbers had halved\n- The term "kulak" was defined loosely and was used to punish anyone who resisted collectivisation\n\n### The Ukrainian Famine (Holodomor) 1932-1933\n\n- Stalin set impossibly high grain quotas for Ukraine\n- When quotas were not met, all grain was confiscated, including seed grain\n- An estimated **3-7 million Ukrainians starved to death**\n- Many historians consider this a deliberate act of genocide (the Holodomor, meaning "death by hunger")\n- The Soviet government denied the famine was happening'),
  q(2, 'What was the term used for the forced removal and persecution of wealthy peasants under Stalin?',
    ['Dekulakisation', 'Collectivisation', 'Industrialisation', 'Nationalisation'], 0,
    'Dekulakisation was Stalin\'s policy of eliminating the kulaks "as a class." Kulaks were wealthy peasants who were targeted because they resisted collectivisation. Millions were deported, sent to labour camps, or killed.'),
  t(3, '## The Five-Year Plans: Rapid Industrialisation\n\nStalin launched a series of **Five-Year Plans** to transform the USSR into a major industrial power:\n\n### First Five-Year Plan (1928-1932)\n- Focus: Heavy industry (steel, coal, iron, electricity, machinery)\n- Targets were set impossibly high; workers who failed to meet them could be punished\n- New industrial cities were built from nothing (e.g., **Magnitogorsk**)\n- Production of coal, iron, and steel increased dramatically\n- Consumer goods were neglected; living standards fell\n\n### Second Five-Year Plan (1933-1937)\n- Continued heavy industry focus but also developed transport and communications\n- The Stakhanovite movement: Alexei Stakhanov allegedly mined 102 tonnes of coal in one shift (14 times the norm); workers who exceeded targets were celebrated as heroes\n\n### Third Five-Year Plan (1938-1941)\n- Shifted focus to armaments as war in Europe approached\n- Disrupted by the purges (loss of managers, engineers, military officers)\n\n### Impact of Industrialisation\n\n| Achievement | Cost |\n|-------------|------|\n| USSR became the 2nd largest industrial power by 1940 | Millions of workers lived in terrible conditions |\n| Coal output increased from 36m to 166m tonnes (1928-1940) | Forced labour (Gulag) built many major projects |\n| Steel output increased from 4m to 18m tonnes | Consumer goods were scarce; quality of life was poor |\n| Literacy campaigns reached millions | Managers and engineers lived in fear of punishment for failure |'),
  fb(4, 'Stalin launched a series of ___-Year Plans to industrialise the USSR. The ___ movement celebrated workers who exceeded production targets, named after a coal miner.',
    ['Five', 'Stakhanovite'],
    'The Five-Year Plans set ambitious targets for industrial production. The Stakhanovite movement was a propaganda campaign celebrating exceptionally productive workers, named after Alexei Stakhanov.'),
  t(5, '## The Purges (1934-1938)\n\nStalin\'s purges were a period of mass political terror that eliminated anyone he perceived as a threat, real or imagined.\n\n### The Murder of Kirov (December 1934)\n\n- **Sergei Kirov**, the popular Leningrad party boss, was assassinated\n- Many historians believe **Stalin ordered the assassination** to create a pretext for the purges\n- Stalin used Kirov\'s death to launch a campaign against alleged conspirators\n\n### The Show Trials (1936-1938)\n\nThree major public trials of senior Bolsheviks:\n\n1. **Trial of the Sixteen (1936)**: Zinoviev and Kamenev were accused of conspiring with Trotsky to murder Kirov and overthrow the state. They confessed (under torture and threats to their families) and were executed.\n2. **Trial of the Seventeen (1937)**: More Old Bolsheviks accused of sabotage and spying for foreign powers.\n3. **Trial of the Twenty-One (1938)**: Bukharin and others accused of being "Trotskyist wreckers." Bukharin was executed.\n\nThe confessions were extracted through **sleep deprivation, torture, threats to family members**, and false promises of leniency.'),
  q(6, 'Why do many historians believe Stalin ordered the assassination of Kirov in 1934?',
    ['To create a pretext for launching the purges against his political enemies', 'Because Kirov was planning a military coup', 'Because Kirov was a secret agent for Nazi Germany', 'Because Kirov had publicly criticised Stalin\'s economic policies'], 0,
    'Kirov was popular and had received more votes than Stalin at the 1934 Party Congress. Many historians believe Stalin saw him as a potential rival and had him murdered, then used the assassination as a pretext to launch the Great Purges against anyone he deemed disloyal.'),
  t(7, '### The Great Terror (Yezhovshchina) 1937-1938\n\nThe purges extended far beyond the show trials:\n\n- The **NKVD** (secret police, led by Nikolai Yezhov) arrested, imprisoned, or executed millions\n- **Military purges**: 3 of 5 marshals, 13 of 15 army commanders, and roughly 35,000 officers were purged \u2014 this severely weakened the Red Army before WWII\n- **Party purges**: Over half of the delegates to the 1934 Party Congress were arrested\n- **Ordinary citizens**: Neighbours, colleagues, and family members denounced each other (often under pressure); quotas were set for arrests in each region\n- The **Gulag** (system of labour camps) held millions of prisoners who worked on construction projects, mines, and railways in brutal conditions\n- An estimated **750,000-1.2 million people were executed** during 1937-1938 alone\n- Total victims of the purges (killed, imprisoned, exiled): estimated at **10-20 million**\n\nIn 1938, Stalin replaced Yezhov with Lavrentiy Beria and had Yezhov himself arrested and executed \u2014 even the chief purger was purged.'),
  fb(8, 'The secret police during the Great Purges were called the ___. The vast system of forced labour camps was known as the ___.',
    ['NKVD', 'Gulag'],
    'The NKVD (People\'s Commissariat for Internal Affairs) carried out the arrests and executions during the Great Terror. The Gulag (Main Administration of Camps) was the system of forced labour camps spread across the Soviet Union, particularly in Siberia.'),
  t(9, '## Women Under Stalin\n\nThe position of women in the Soviet Union changed dramatically under communist rule:\n\n### Early Bolshevik Reforms (1917-1920s)\n- Women gained equal legal rights, including the right to vote and own property\n- Divorce was made easy; abortion was legalised (1920)\n- The **Zhenotdel** (Women\'s Department) promoted women\'s rights and literacy\n- Women were encouraged to enter the workforce\n\n### Under Stalin (1930s)\n- Stalin reversed many early reforms in the interest of increasing the population and strengthening the family unit\n- **Abortion was banned** (1936); divorce became more difficult and expensive\n- Families with many children received state awards: the **"Mother Heroine"** medal was given to women with 10 or more children\n- Women were expected to be both workers AND mothers (the "double burden")\n- Women entered heavy industry and professions in large numbers \u2014 by 1940, women were 39% of the industrial workforce\n- However, women rarely reached senior political positions; the party leadership remained overwhelmingly male\n\n### Assessment\n\nCommunism brought genuine improvements in women\'s education, literacy, and workforce participation. However, under Stalin, women were instrumentalised \u2014 their rights were expanded or restricted based on state needs, not genuine equality.'),
  q(10, 'What was the "double burden" that Soviet women experienced under Stalin?',
    ['They were expected to work full-time in industry while also fulfilling traditional roles as mothers and homemakers', 'They had to serve in the army and work in factories simultaneously', 'They were forced to work on collective farms and in mines', 'They had to attend political meetings and work double shifts'], 0,
    'Under Stalin, women were mobilised into the industrial workforce in large numbers but were also pressured to have many children and maintain the household. This "double burden" of paid work and unpaid domestic labour meant women worked significantly more hours than men.'),
  t(11, '## The Coming of World War II\n\nBy the late 1930s, the threat of Nazi Germany shaped Soviet policy:\n\n- Stalin sought to buy time through diplomacy\n- In August 1939, the USSR and Nazi Germany signed the **Nazi-Soviet Pact (Molotov-Ribbentrop Pact)**:\n  - A non-aggression agreement; secret clauses divided Eastern Europe into spheres of influence\n  - Shocked the world \u2014 fascism and communism were supposed to be mortal enemies\n- On **22 June 1941**, Hitler launched **Operation Barbarossa**, the invasion of the USSR, breaking the pact\n- Stalin was initially paralysed by shock; the Red Army suffered catastrophic losses (partly due to the military purges)\n- The USSR would go on to bear the heaviest cost of WWII: an estimated **27 million Soviet citizens died**\n\n### Reflection\n\nThe story of communism in Russia from 1900 to 1940 is one of radical transformation: from Tsarist autocracy, through revolution, civil war, and the creation of a totalitarian state under Stalin. The ideals of Marx were adapted and often betrayed in practice. The human cost was immense: famines, purges, forced labour, and eventually the devastation of World War II.'),
  q(12, 'Why was the Nazi-Soviet Pact of August 1939 so shocking to the world?',
    ['Because fascism and communism were supposed to be ideological enemies', 'Because Germany and Russia had always been allies', 'Because it guaranteed peace in Europe forever', 'Because Britain and France had approved the agreement'], 0,
    'The Nazi-Soviet Pact shocked the world because Nazi Germany and the Soviet Union represented opposing ideologies (fascism vs communism) that were supposedly irreconcilable. The pact was a cynical deal: both sides were buying time and dividing Eastern Europe between them.'),
];

