// --- Lesson 1: The American Dream and the Roaring Twenties ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch2_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## The American Dream and the Rise of Capitalism\n\nWhile Russia was pursuing communism, the United States represented the opposite end of the ideological spectrum: **capitalism**.\n\n### What is Capitalism?\n\nCapitalism is an economic system based on:\n1. **Private ownership** of the means of production (factories, land, businesses)\n2. **Free market** competition (supply and demand set prices)\n3. **Profit motive** (individuals and companies seek to maximise profit)\n4. **Limited government intervention** in the economy (laissez-faire)\n5. **Individual freedom** and the right to accumulate wealth\n\n### The American Dream\n\nThe **American Dream** is the belief that anyone, regardless of their background, can achieve success and prosperity through hard work and determination. It is deeply tied to capitalism \u2014 the idea that individual effort will be rewarded in a free market.\n\nHowever, the American Dream was not equally accessible to all. In the early 20th century:\n- **African Americans** faced racial segregation (Jim Crow laws) and were denied equal opportunities\n- **Women** had limited economic and political rights (they gained the vote only in 1920)\n- **Immigrants** often faced discrimination and worked in exploitative conditions\n- **Native Americans** were marginalised on reservations'),
  q(2, 'Which of the following is NOT a characteristic of capitalism?',
    ['State ownership of all means of production', 'Private ownership of businesses', 'Free market competition', 'The profit motive'], 0,
    'State ownership of the means of production is a characteristic of communism, not capitalism. Capitalism is defined by private ownership, free markets, competition, and the profit motive.'),
  t(3, '## The Capitalist Boom of the 1920s (The Roaring Twenties)\n\nThe 1920s in America were a period of extraordinary economic growth and cultural change.\n\n### Reasons for the Economic Boom\n\n1. **World War I profits**: The USA sold weapons, food, and raw materials to Europe during WWI; European countries owed America billions in war debts\n2. **Mass production**: Henry Ford perfected the **assembly line** technique, making goods (especially cars) affordable. By 1929, there were **26 million cars** in America\n3. **New consumer goods**: Radios, refrigerators, vacuum cleaners, and washing machines became widely available\n4. **Buying on credit**: "Hire purchase" (instalment plans) allowed people to buy goods they could not afford outright\n5. **Stock market speculation**: Millions of Americans bought shares, often **on the margin** (borrowing money to invest)\n6. **Advertising**: New mass media (radio, magazines, cinema) created consumer desire\n7. **Republican government policies**: Low taxes, high tariffs (to protect American industry), and minimal regulation\n\n### Cultural Changes\n\n- **Jazz Age**: African American musicians like Louis Armstrong and Duke Ellington transformed popular music\n- **Flappers**: Young women challenged traditional norms with short hair, short skirts, smoking, and dancing\n- **Prohibition** (1920-1933): The ban on alcohol led to speakeasies, organised crime (Al Capone), and widespread lawbreaking\n- **Hollywood**: The film industry boomed; movies became mass entertainment'),
  fb(4, 'Henry Ford revolutionised manufacturing by perfecting the ___ line technique. Many Americans bought shares "on the ___," meaning they borrowed money to invest.',
    ['assembly', 'margin'],
    'Ford\'s assembly line made mass production possible, dramatically reducing the cost of goods like cars. Buying "on the margin" meant investors put down only 10% of a share\'s value and borrowed the rest, which was extremely risky.'),
  t(5, '### Who Did NOT Share in the Boom?\n\nThe prosperity of the 1920s was uneven:\n\n| Group | Situation |\n|-------|----------|\n| **Farmers** | Overproduction drove down crop prices; many went bankrupt; farm income fell throughout the 1920s |\n| **African Americans** | Faced Jim Crow segregation in the South; many migrated north (the Great Migration) but still faced discrimination |\n| **Industrial workers** | Wages did not keep pace with profits; trade unions were weak; working conditions were often dangerous |\n| **Older industries** | Coal, textiles, and railways declined as new industries grew |\n| **Native Americans** | Poverty and marginalisation on reservations |\n\n**The wealth gap**: By 1929, the richest **5% of Americans** earned **33% of all income**. This inequality would prove to be a fundamental weakness of the boom.'),
  q(6, 'Which group experienced economic hardship throughout the 1920s despite the general boom?',
    ['Farmers, due to overproduction and falling crop prices', 'Factory owners in the automobile industry', 'Wall Street stockbrokers', 'Hollywood film producers'], 0,
    'American farmers did not share in the prosperity of the 1920s. Overproduction (partly caused by mechanisation) drove crop prices down. Many farmers went bankrupt and lost their land. This agricultural depression foreshadowed the wider economic collapse that was to come.'),
  t(7, '## Comparing Capitalism and Communism in the 1920s\n\nBy the late 1920s, the USA and USSR represented two radically different visions of how society should be organised:\n\n| Feature | USA (Capitalism) | USSR (Communism under NEP) |\n|---------|------------------|---------------------------|\n| **Ownership** | Private; individuals own businesses | State owns major industry; limited private enterprise |\n| **Political system** | Democracy (though limited by racism) | One-party state; no political opposition |\n| **Individual rights** | Constitution protects individual freedoms | Rights subordinate to the state |\n| **Economy** | Free market; boom and bust cycles | Centrally planned (moving to Five-Year Plans) |\n| **Wealth distribution** | Highly unequal | More equal but widespread poverty |\n| **Culture** | Consumer culture; jazz, cinema, flappers | State-controlled culture; socialist realism |\n\nBoth systems had significant strengths and weaknesses. The 1930s would test both severely \u2014 capitalism would face the Great Depression while communism faced Stalin\u2019s brutal transformation.'),
  q(8, 'What was a key weakness of the American economic boom of the 1920s?',
    ['Wealth was very unevenly distributed, with the richest 5% earning 33% of all income', 'There was too much government regulation of business', 'The economy was growing too slowly', 'All Americans shared equally in the prosperity'], 0,
    'The extreme wealth inequality meant that most Americans could not afford to keep buying the goods that factories were producing. This overproduction and underconsumption was a fundamental cause of the economic crash that followed.'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: Wall Street Crash, Great Depression, and the New Deal ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch2_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## The Wall Street Crash of October 1929\n\nThe prosperity of the 1920s came to a catastrophic end in October 1929 when the American stock market collapsed.\n\n### Causes of the Crash\n\n1. **Overproduction**: Factories produced more goods than people could buy; warehouses filled up\n2. **Underconsumption**: Wages were too low and wealth too unequal for ordinary Americans to sustain demand\n3. **Speculation**: Millions had bought shares "on the margin" (with borrowed money), driving prices to unsustainable levels\n4. **Weak banking system**: Banks had also speculated; many had lent recklessly\n5. **Agricultural depression**: Farmers had been struggling throughout the 1920s\n6. **International factors**: European economies were weak; high US tariffs reduced international trade\n\n### The Crash\n\n- **24 October 1929 ("Black Thursday")**: Panic selling began; 13 million shares were traded\n- **29 October 1929 ("Black Tuesday")**: The market collapsed completely; 16 million shares were dumped\n- Share prices lost **$30 billion** in value in two days (equivalent to over $400 billion today)\n- Millions of investors were ruined; those who had bought on the margin lost everything\n- Banks that had invested in the stock market began to fail'),
  q(2, 'What does it mean that many Americans bought shares "on the margin" before the 1929 Crash?',
    ['They borrowed money to buy shares, paying only a fraction of the share price upfront', 'They bought shares using their savings accounts', 'They purchased shares that were guaranteed by the government', 'They only bought shares in well-established companies'], 0,
    'Buying "on the margin" meant an investor could purchase $1,000 worth of shares by putting down just $100 and borrowing the remaining $900 from a broker. If share prices fell, the investor still owed the full amount. When the crash came, millions were left with huge debts and worthless shares.'),
  t(3, '## The Great Depression (1929-1939)\n\nThe Wall Street Crash triggered the worst economic depression in modern history.\n\n### Impact on America\n\n| Indicator | 1929 | 1933 (worst year) |\n|-----------|------|-------------------|\n| **Unemployment** | 3% | 25% (13 million people) |\n| **Industrial production** | 100 (index) | 54 (nearly halved) |\n| **Bank failures** | 659 | 4,000+ (in 1933 alone) |\n| **Farm income** | $6 billion | $2 billion |\n| **Homelessness** | Minimal | Millions; "Hoovervilles" (shantytowns) |\n\n### The Human Cost\n\n- Families lost their homes and savings when banks failed (there was no deposit insurance)\n- **Hoovervilles**: Shantytowns named mockingly after President Herbert Hoover, who was seen as doing nothing to help\n- **Dust Bowl**: Severe drought in the Great Plains combined with poor farming practices created massive dust storms; thousands of farming families migrated west to California\n- Malnutrition and preventable diseases increased; some people starved\n- Psychological impact: loss of dignity, family breakdown, increased suicide rates\n- **African Americans** and other minorities were hit hardest \u2014 "last hired, first fired"'),
  fb(4, 'Shantytowns built by homeless people during the Depression were called ___, named after the president who was blamed for the crisis. By 1933, unemployment reached ___% in the USA.',
    ['Hoovervilles', '25'],
    'Hoovervilles were makeshift camps of shacks and tents, named after President Herbert Hoover. By 1933, one in four American workers was unemployed \u2014 approximately 13 million people.'),
  t(5, '## Franklin Delano Roosevelt and the New Deal\n\nIn the 1932 presidential election, **Franklin Delano Roosevelt (FDR)** defeated Hoover in a landslide. He promised Americans a **"New Deal"** \u2014 a programme of government intervention to end the Depression.\n\n### The First New Deal (1933-1934)\n\nFDR took office on 4 March 1933. In his first **"Hundred Days"**, he pushed through a wave of legislation:\n\n| Agency/Act | Purpose |\n|-----------|----------|\n| **Emergency Banking Act** | Closed all banks temporarily; only sound banks allowed to reopen; restored public confidence |\n| **Federal Emergency Relief Administration (FERA)** | $500 million in direct relief to the unemployed |\n| **Civilian Conservation Corps (CCC)** | Employed 2.5 million young men in environmental projects (planting trees, building parks) |\n| **Agricultural Adjustment Act (AAA)** | Paid farmers to reduce production, raising crop prices |\n| **National Industrial Recovery Act (NIRA)** | Set fair competition codes; established workers\' right to unionise |\n| **Tennessee Valley Authority (TVA)** | Built dams to provide electricity, flood control, and jobs in the impoverished Tennessee Valley |\n| **Public Works Administration (PWA)** | Built roads, bridges, hospitals, schools ($3.3 billion in projects) |\n\nFDR also used **"fireside chats"** \u2014 radio broadcasts \u2014 to explain his policies directly to the American people, building trust and confidence.'),
  q(6, 'What was the purpose of FDR\'s "fireside chats"?',
    ['To explain his policies directly to the American people via radio broadcasts', 'To hold meetings with his cabinet beside a fireplace', 'To negotiate with foreign leaders', 'To broadcast military orders during wartime'], 0,
    'FDR used radio to speak directly to millions of Americans in their homes. These "fireside chats" helped restore confidence, explain complex policies in simple terms, and build a personal connection between the president and ordinary citizens.'),
  t(7, '### The Second New Deal (1935-1938)\n\nFDR expanded his programme with more ambitious reforms:\n\n| Act/Agency | Purpose |\n|-----------|----------|\n| **Works Progress Administration (WPA)** | The largest New Deal agency; employed 8.5 million people building roads, schools, airports, and supporting arts projects |\n| **Social Security Act (1935)** | Established pensions for the elderly, unemployment insurance, and aid for the disabled \u2014 the foundation of the American welfare state |\n| **Wagner Act (1935)** | Strengthened workers\' right to unionise and bargain collectively; established the National Labor Relations Board |\n| **Fair Labor Standards Act (1938)** | Set a national minimum wage and maximum 44-hour work week; banned child labour |\n\n### Opposition to the New Deal\n\n| From the Right | From the Left |\n|---------------|---------------|\n| Business leaders said it was too much government interference | Radicals like Huey Long ("Share Our Wealth") said it did not go far enough |\n| The Supreme Court struck down some New Deal laws as unconstitutional (NIRA, AAA) | Socialists and communists argued capitalism itself was the problem |\n| Republicans accused FDR of moving towards socialism | Trade unions wanted even stronger protections |\n| Liberty League: wealthy opponents called the New Deal "un-American" | Father Coughlin initially supported FDR but turned against him |'),
  fb(8, 'The ___ Act of 1935 established pensions for the elderly and unemployment insurance, forming the foundation of the American welfare state. The largest New Deal employment agency was the ___ (WPA).',
    ['Social Security', 'Works Progress Administration'],
    'The Social Security Act (1935) was one of FDR\'s most enduring achievements, creating a safety net that still exists today. The WPA employed 8.5 million Americans in public works projects between 1935 and 1943.'),
  t(9, '## Assessment of the New Deal\n\n### Successes\n\n- Restored confidence and hope ("the only thing we have to fear is fear itself")\n- Unemployment fell from 25% (1933) to 14% (1937)\n- Created vital infrastructure: roads, bridges, schools, hospitals, dams, airports\n- Established lasting reforms: Social Security, bank deposit insurance (FDIC), Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC)\n- Strengthened trade unions and workers\' rights\n- Preserved American democracy at a time when many countries turned to fascism or communism\n\n### Limitations\n\n- Did NOT end the Great Depression \u2014 unemployment was still 17% in 1939; it was **World War II** that finally ended mass unemployment\n- African Americans received unequal treatment; New Deal agencies often discriminated; FDR refused to support anti-lynching legislation to avoid losing Southern Democratic support\n- Women were marginalised in many New Deal programmes\n- The 1937 "Roosevelt Recession" showed the economy was not yet self-sustaining (FDR cut spending too soon)\n- Deficit spending worried fiscal conservatives\n- Some programmes were temporary relief, not structural change\n\n### SA Context\n\nThe Great Depression hit South Africa hard, particularly agriculture. The economic crisis contributed to poor white poverty and the growth of Afrikaner nationalism, as white workers competed with black workers for scarce jobs. The government\'s response included segregationist labour policies that foreshadowed apartheid.'),
  q(10, 'What ultimately ended the Great Depression in the United States?',
    ['The massive government spending and industrial mobilisation for World War II', 'The New Deal programmes of FDR', 'A natural recovery of the free market', 'Foreign aid from European countries'], 0,
    'While the New Deal reduced unemployment and restored confidence, it did not end the Depression. It was the enormous industrial mobilisation for World War II (from 1941) that finally ended mass unemployment and returned the economy to full production.'),
];

