// =============================================================================

// --- Lesson 1: Race Hierarchies, Eugenics, and the Human Genome Project ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch3_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## Notions of Race Hierarchies in the 19th Century\n\nThe concept of "race" as a biological category used to rank human beings was a product of European colonialism and pseudo-science in the 18th and 19th centuries.\n\n### The Development of "Scientific" Racism\n\n- **Carl Linnaeus** (1707-1778): Swedish botanist who classified humans into four races with attributed characteristics (Europeans as "inventive," Africans as "lazy" and "negligent")\n- **Johann Blumenbach** (1752-1840): German anatomist who coined the term "Caucasian" and ranked five races in a hierarchy with Europeans at the top\n- **Social Darwinism**: Charles Darwin\'s theory of evolution was misapplied to human societies by thinkers like **Herbert Spencer** ("survival of the fittest"). This was used to argue that European domination of other peoples was "natural"\n- **Phrenology and craniometry**: Pseudo-scientists measured skulls and other physical features to "prove" racial hierarchies\n\n### Why Were These Ideas Developed?\n\n1. **Justification for colonialism**: Europeans needed a moral justification for conquering and exploiting other peoples\n2. **Justification for slavery**: If Africans were "inferior," enslaving them could be portrayed as acceptable or even beneficial\n3. **Economic interests**: Racial hierarchies served the economic interests of European colonial powers\n4. **Power**: Classification was a tool of domination \u2014 the classifier placed themselves at the top'),
  q(2, 'Why was Social Darwinism used by European colonial powers?',
    ['To justify European domination of other peoples as a natural outcome of evolution', 'To promote equality among all races', 'To argue against colonialism', 'To prove that all humans shared a common ancestor'], 0,
    'Social Darwinism misapplied Darwin\'s theory of natural selection to argue that European dominance over other peoples was the result of "natural" superiority. This "survival of the fittest" argument was used to justify colonialism, exploitation, and racial inequality.'),
  t(3, '## Eugenics: The "Science" of Racial Improvement\n\n**Eugenics** was the pseudo-scientific movement that aimed to "improve" the human race through selective breeding.\n\n- Founded by **Francis Galton** (1822-1911), a cousin of Charles Darwin\n- Galton argued that desirable traits (intelligence, health, morality) were hereditary and could be bred into the population\n- **Positive eugenics**: Encouraging "desirable" people (usually white, middle-class, educated) to have more children\n- **Negative eugenics**: Preventing "undesirable" people from reproducing through forced sterilisation, institutionalisation, or immigration restrictions\n\n### Eugenics in Practice\n\n| Country | Eugenics Policies |\n|---------|-------------------|\n| **USA** | Forced sterilisation laws in 30+ states; over 60,000 people forcibly sterilised; targeted the mentally ill, disabled, poor, and racial minorities |\n| **Australia** | Stolen Generations: Aboriginal children forcibly removed from families and placed with white families |\n| **Namibia** | German colonial genocide of the Herero and Nama people (1904-1908); concentration camps; medical experiments |\n| **South Africa** | Racial classification and segregation based on pseudo-scientific racial categories |\n| **Nazi Germany** | Taken to its extreme: sterilisation, euthanasia (T-4 programme), and ultimately the Holocaust |\n\nEugenics was NOT fringe science \u2014 it was mainstream in many countries, supported by universities, governments, and prominent scientists until the horrors of Nazi Germany discredited it.'),
  fb(4, 'The eugenics movement was founded by ___, a cousin of Charles Darwin. Negative eugenics involved preventing "undesirable" people from reproducing through forced ___.',
    ['Francis Galton', 'sterilisation'],
    'Francis Galton coined the term "eugenics" (meaning "well-born") and believed that human qualities could be improved through selective breeding. Forced sterilisation was the most extreme form of negative eugenics, practised in the USA, Scandinavia, and Nazi Germany.'),
  t(5, '## Race and Eugenics: Case Studies\n\n### USA: Eugenics and Racial Segregation\n\n- The USA was a **pioneer of eugenics** \u2014 the first forced sterilisation law was passed in Indiana in 1907\n- In **Buck v. Bell (1927)**, the US Supreme Court upheld forced sterilisation, with Justice Oliver Wendell Holmes declaring: *"Three generations of imbeciles are enough"*\n- **Immigration Act of 1924**: Restricted immigration from Southern and Eastern Europe and banned most Asian immigration \u2014 motivated by eugenics ideas about "racial purity"\n- African Americans were disproportionately targeted for forced sterilisation, especially in the South\n- The **Tuskegee Syphilis Study (1932-1972)**: African American men with syphilis were deliberately left untreated so doctors could study the disease\u2019s progression \u2014 a horrific example of medical racism\n\n### Australia: The Stolen Generations\n\n- From approximately **1910 to 1970**, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander children were forcibly removed from their families by government agencies\n- The policy aimed to "breed out" Indigenous culture and assimilate children into white Australian society\n- Children were placed in institutions or with white families; forbidden to speak their languages or practise their culture\n- The **Bringing Them Home Report (1997)** documented the devastating impact and recommended an official apology\n- Prime Minister **Kevin Rudd** issued a formal apology in **February 2008**'),
  q(6, 'What was the purpose of Australia\'s policy of removing Aboriginal children from their families?',
    ['To "breed out" Indigenous culture and assimilate Aboriginal people into white society', 'To provide better education for Aboriginal children', 'To protect children from poverty on reserves', 'To train children for military service'], 0,
    'The Stolen Generations policy was a deliberate attempt at cultural genocide. By removing children from their families and culture, the government aimed to eliminate Aboriginal identity. The 1997 Bringing Them Home Report found the policy caused immense trauma lasting generations.'),
  t(7, '### Namibia: The Herero and Nama Genocide (1904-1908)\n\n- Germany colonised South West Africa (modern Namibia) in the 1880s\n- German settlers seized Herero and Nama land and cattle\n- In 1904, the Herero people rose up against German colonial rule\n- General **Lothar von Trotha** issued the **extermination order (Vernichtungsbefehl)**: *"Every Herero, with or without a gun, with or without cattle, will be shot"*\n- The Herero were driven into the **Omaheke Desert** where thousands died of thirst\n- Survivors were placed in **concentration camps** (the term was already in use) where they were used for forced labour\n- **Eugen Fischer** conducted racial experiments on prisoners, later influencing Nazi racial ideology\n- An estimated **65,000 Herero** (80% of the population) and **10,000 Nama** (50%) were killed\n- Germany formally recognised this as a **genocide** in 2021 and offered financial support (though the amount was criticised as inadequate)\n\n### South Africa: Race and Eugenics\n\n- South Africa\u2019s system of racial classification drew on pseudo-scientific ideas of racial hierarchy\n- The Population Registration Act (1950) classified every South African by race: White, Coloured, Indian, or Native (later Bantu/Black)\n- These classifications determined every aspect of life under apartheid\n- The "pencil test" and other degrading methods were used to classify people of ambiguous race'),
  q(8, 'Why is the Herero and Nama genocide (1904-1908) significant in the history of eugenics and racism?',
    ['It was the first genocide of the 20th century, and German scientists conducted racial experiments on survivors', 'It was the largest genocide in African history', 'It was committed by African people against Europeans', 'It was immediately condemned by all European nations'], 0,
    'The Herero and Nama genocide is considered the first genocide of the 20th century. Eugen Fischer\'s racial experiments in concentration camps in Namibia later influenced Nazi racial ideology. The connection between colonial violence and the Holocaust is a key historical debate.'),
  t(9, '## The Modern Understanding of Race: The Human Genome Project\n\nThe **Human Genome Project** (1990-2003) was a landmark scientific effort that mapped the entire human genetic code. Its findings fundamentally challenged the concept of biological race:\n\n### Key Findings\n\n1. **All humans share 99.9% of their DNA** \u2014 the genetic differences between any two people are tiny\n2. **There is more genetic variation within so-called racial groups than between them** \u2014 two people of different "races" may be more genetically similar than two people of the same "race"\n3. **Race is a social construct, not a biological reality** \u2014 there are no genes that define discrete racial groups\n4. **Physical differences** (skin colour, hair texture, facial features) reflect adaptations to different environments over thousands of years, not fundamental biological differences\n5. **All humans share a common African origin** \u2014 modern humans evolved in Africa and migrated outward\n\n### Implications\n\n- The pseudo-scientific basis for racism has been **completely debunked** by modern genetics\n- Race continues to have enormous **social significance** even though it has no biological basis\n- The legacy of racist ideas continues to shape societies worldwide, including South Africa\n- Understanding that race is a social construct is essential for combating racism'),
  fb(10, 'The Human Genome Project found that all humans share ___% of their DNA. Modern science has shown that race is a ___ construct, not a biological reality.',
    ['99.9', 'social'],
    'The Human Genome Project conclusively demonstrated that the genetic differences between humans are minimal (0.1% variation) and do not correspond to traditional racial categories. Race is now understood as a social and political construct with no basis in biology.'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: Case Study - Nazi Germany and the Holocaust ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch3_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## Nazi Germany and the Holocaust: Eugenics Taken to Its Extreme\n\nThe Holocaust represents the most horrific application of racial ideology and eugenics in human history. The systematic murder of **six million Jews** and millions of others by Nazi Germany demonstrates where ideas of racial hierarchy can ultimately lead.\n\n### The Nazi Racial Ideology\n\nAdolf Hitler and the Nazi Party built their entire ideology on racial pseudo-science:\n\n- **Aryan master race**: The Nazis believed Germanic peoples ("Aryans") were a superior race destined to rule\n- **Racial hierarchy**: They ranked races with "Aryans" at the top and Jews, Roma, Slavs, and Africans at the bottom\n- **Social Darwinism**: History was seen as a struggle between races; the "strong" would naturally dominate the "weak"\n- **Anti-Semitism**: Jews were portrayed as a parasitic race that was secretly controlling and destroying "Aryan" civilisation\n- **Lebensraum**: "Living space" \u2014 the idea that Germany needed to conquer territory in Eastern Europe for the "Aryan" race to expand\n\nHitler outlined these ideas in **Mein Kampf** ("My Struggle"), written in prison in 1924.'),
  q(2, 'What did the Nazi concept of "Lebensraum" refer to?',
    ['The idea that Germany needed to conquer territory in Eastern Europe for the Aryan race to expand', 'A type of concentration camp', 'The name of the Nazi racial classification system', 'A peace treaty between Germany and the Soviet Union'], 0,
    'Lebensraum ("living space") was the Nazi belief that the German people needed more territory for their growing population. Hitler argued this space should be taken from Eastern Europe and the Soviet Union, whose Slavic inhabitants were considered "racially inferior."'),
  t(3, '### Stages of Persecution (1933-1939)\n\nThe persecution of Jews and others escalated gradually:\n\n| Date | Event |\n|------|-------|\n| **1933** | Boycott of Jewish businesses; Jews dismissed from civil service; book burnings |\n| **1935** | **Nuremberg Laws**: stripped Jews of citizenship; banned marriage and sexual relations between Jews and "Aryans" |\n| **1936** | Anti-Jewish measures temporarily eased during Berlin Olympics (to avoid international criticism) |\n| **1938** | **Kristallnacht** ("Night of Broken Glass", 9-10 November): Nazi-organised pogrom; 91 Jews killed; 30,000 arrested; synagogues and businesses destroyed |\n| **1939** | Jews forced into ghettos in occupied Poland; required to wear the yellow Star of David |\n\n### The T-4 Euthanasia Programme (1939-1941)\n\n- Before targeting Jews for mass murder, the Nazis first practised on disabled Germans\n- The **T-4 programme** killed approximately **70,000 mentally and physically disabled people** in gas chambers disguised as shower rooms\n- Families were told their relatives had died of natural causes\n- Public protests (especially from the Catholic Church) forced the programme underground, but killings continued secretly\n- The technology and personnel from T-4 were later transferred to the Holocaust death camps'),
  fb(4, 'The ___ Laws of 1935 stripped Jews of German citizenship. On the night of 9-10 November 1938, the Nazi-organised pogrom known as ___ destroyed Jewish businesses and synagogues.',
    ['Nuremberg', 'Kristallnacht'],
    'The Nuremberg Laws (1935) were the legal foundation of Nazi anti-Semitism, defining who was Jewish and stripping them of all rights. Kristallnacht ("Night of Broken Glass") marked a dramatic escalation from legal discrimination to outright violence.'),
  t(5, '### The Holocaust (1941-1945): The "Final Solution"\n\nThe Nazi regime moved from persecution to systematic extermination:\n\n**The Wannsee Conference (January 1942)**\n- Senior Nazi officials met to coordinate the "Final Solution to the Jewish Question"\n- They planned the systematic deportation of all European Jews to extermination camps\n- The language was euphemistic: "special treatment" meant murder; "resettlement" meant deportation to death camps\n\n**The Death Camps**\n- Six main extermination camps were built in occupied Poland: **Auschwitz-Birkenau**, Treblinka, Sobibor, Belzec, Chelmno, and Majdanek\n- At Auschwitz alone, approximately **1.1 million people were murdered**, the vast majority Jews\n- Victims arrived in cattle trucks; at the ramp, SS doctors conducted "selections" \u2014 those deemed unfit for work were sent directly to gas chambers\n- Bodies were cremated in industrial ovens\n- Those selected for work were used as slave labour until they died of exhaustion, starvation, or disease\n\n**Einsatzgruppen (Mobile Killing Squads)**\n- Behind the advancing German army in the Soviet Union, special SS units shot Jews, Roma, and Soviet officials\n- Over **1.5 million people** were killed by Einsatzgruppen, often in mass shootings at pits (e.g., **Babi Yar**, Ukraine: 33,771 Jews killed in two days)'),
  q(6, 'What was decided at the Wannsee Conference of January 1942?',
    ['The systematic deportation and extermination of all European Jews (the "Final Solution")', 'The invasion of the Soviet Union', 'The creation of the Nuremberg Laws', 'A peace agreement with the Allied powers'], 0,
    'The Wannsee Conference coordinated the logistics of the "Final Solution to the Jewish Question" \u2014 the systematic murder of all Jews in Nazi-occupied Europe. Senior officials discussed how to transport, concentrate, and kill millions of people with industrial efficiency.'),
  t(7, '### Other Victims of the Holocaust\n\nWhile Jews were the primary target, the Nazis also systematically persecuted and murdered:\n\n| Group | Estimated Deaths |\n|-------|------------------|\n| **Roma and Sinti** | 200,000-500,000 (the "Porajmos" or "Devouring") |\n| **Soviet prisoners of war** | 3.3 million died in captivity |\n| **Poles** | 1.8-2 million civilians |\n| **Disabled people** | 250,000+ (T-4 and beyond) |\n| **Homosexuals** | 5,000-15,000 in camps (pink triangle) |\n| **Jehovah\'s Witnesses** | 1,000-2,500 |\n| **Political opponents** | Communists, Social Democrats, trade unionists |\n\n### Resistance\n\nDespite impossible conditions, there was resistance:\n- **Warsaw Ghetto Uprising (April-May 1943)**: Jews fought back against deportation to Treblinka; held out for nearly a month\n- **Sobibor and Treblinka uprisings (1943)**: Prisoners revolted and some escaped\n- **Sonderkommando revolt at Auschwitz (1944)**: Prisoners destroyed one crematorium\n- **Partisans**: Jewish and non-Jewish resistance fighters operated behind German lines\n- **Righteous Among the Nations**: Non-Jews who risked their lives to save Jews (e.g., Oskar Schindler, Raoul Wallenberg)'),
  fb(8, 'The mass shooting at ___ in Ukraine in September 1941 killed 33,771 Jews in two days. The ___ Ghetto Uprising of 1943 was the largest act of Jewish resistance during the Holocaust.',
    ['Babi Yar', 'Warsaw'],
    'Babi Yar was one of the largest single massacres of the Holocaust, carried out by Einsatzgruppen. The Warsaw Ghetto Uprising (April-May 1943) saw Jewish fighters hold out against the German army for nearly a month in a desperate act of resistance.'),
  t(9, '## Lessons of the Holocaust and Relevance to South Africa\n\n### Why Study the Holocaust?\n\n1. **It shows where racial ideology leads**: The Holocaust was the logical endpoint of centuries of racial pseudo-science and anti-Semitism\n2. **It was not inevitable**: Each stage of escalation involved choices by individuals, institutions, and governments\n3. **Ordinary people participated**: The Holocaust was not carried out only by fanatics but also by ordinary soldiers, civil servants, and railway workers\n4. **Bystanders matter**: Those who did nothing enabled the genocide; those who acted saved lives\n\n### Connections to South Africa\n\n- South Africa\u2019s **racial classification system** (Population Registration Act, 1950) drew on the same pseudo-scientific ideas about race that informed Nazi policy\n- The architect of apartheid, **Hendrik Verwoerd**, had studied in Germany in the 1920s\n- Some South African Afrikaners sympathised with Nazi Germany during WWII (e.g., the **Ossewabrandwag**)\n- The **Holocaust challenged the world** to take racism seriously; yet apartheid was established in 1948, just three years after the liberation of Auschwitz\n- South Africa\u2019s Truth and Reconciliation Commission was influenced by post-Holocaust concepts of transitional justice\n- The Constitution of democratic South Africa (1996) explicitly guarantees equality and non-discrimination, partly informed by the lessons of the Holocaust'),
  q(10, 'How does the Holocaust connect to South African history?',
    ['South Africa\'s racial classification system drew on similar pseudo-scientific ideas about race, and some Afrikaners sympathised with Nazi Germany', 'South Africa participated in the Holocaust directly', 'The Holocaust had no connection to South African history', 'Nazi Germany colonised South Africa'], 0,
    'The connection is ideological: both the Holocaust and apartheid were rooted in pseudo-scientific ideas about racial hierarchy. The timing is also significant \u2014 apartheid was established in 1948, just three years after the world learned about the Holocaust. This shows how racial ideology persisted even after its most extreme consequences were revealed.'),
];

