// --- Lesson 1: African Nationalism (SANNC/ANC, Atlantic Charter, Freedom Charter) ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch4_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## Origins of Nationalism\n\nNationalism is the belief that a group of people who share a common identity (language, culture, history, territory) should govern themselves as an independent nation. In the 20th century, nationalism took many forms:\n\n- **Anti-colonial nationalism**: Colonised peoples fighting for independence from European empires\n- **Ethnic nationalism**: Based on shared ethnicity or "race" (can be exclusionary and dangerous)\n- **Civic nationalism**: Based on shared citizenship and political values (inclusive)\n\nIn the South African context, two major forms of nationalism developed in parallel: **African nationalism** and **Afrikaner nationalism**. Understanding both is essential for CAPS Grade 11 History.\n\n## African Nationalism: The Struggle for Rights and Freedom\n\n### The Formation of the SANNC/ANC\n\nThe **South African Native National Congress (SANNC)** was founded on **8 January 1912** in Bloemfontein. It was renamed the **African National Congress (ANC)** in 1923.\n\n**Founders and early leaders:**\n- **Pixley ka Isaka Seme**: Lawyer who called for African unity; key organiser of the founding conference\n- **John Langalibalele Dube**: First president of the SANNC; educator and journalist\n- **Sol Plaatje**: First Secretary-General; writer and intellectual'),
  q(2, 'In what year was the South African Native National Congress (SANNC) founded?',
    ['1912', '1923', '1948', '1902'], 0,
    'The SANNC was founded on 8 January 1912 in Bloemfontein. It was the first national political organisation for black South Africans, bringing together chiefs, intellectuals, and ordinary people to fight for their rights. It was renamed the ANC in 1923.'),
  t(3, '### Early Campaigns and the 1913 Natives Land Act\n\nThe SANNC\'s first major challenge was the **Natives Land Act of 1913**, which:\n- Restricted African land ownership to just **7.3%** of South Africa\'s total land area (later increased to 13% by the 1936 Native Trust and Land Act)\n- Made it illegal for Africans to buy or rent land outside these "reserves"\n- Forced thousands of African sharecroppers and tenant farmers off the land\n\nSol Plaatje documented the devastating impact in his book **Native Life in South Africa** (1916): *"Awakening on Friday morning, June 20, 1913, the South African native found himself, not actually a slave, but a pariah in the land of his birth."*\n\n### Methods of the Early ANC\n\nThe early ANC used **moderate, constitutional methods**:\n- Petitions to the British government and South African parliament\n- Delegations to London (1914, 1919) to appeal for justice \u2014 they were ignored\n- Newspaper campaigns and public meetings\n- Legal challenges\n\nThese polite methods achieved very little, leading to growing frustration among younger members.'),
  fb(4, 'The Natives Land Act of ___ restricted African land ownership to just 7.3% of South Africa. Sol ___ documented its impact in his book Native Life in South Africa.',
    ['1913', 'Plaatje'],
    'The 1913 Natives Land Act was one of the most devastating pieces of legislation in South African history. Sol Plaatje was one of the first to document its effects, travelling across the country to record the suffering of displaced African families.'),
  t(5, '## The Atlantic Charter and Its Impact on African Nationalism\n\nThe **Atlantic Charter** was a joint statement issued by US President Franklin Roosevelt and British Prime Minister Winston Churchill in **August 1941** (during WWII, before the USA officially entered the war).\n\n### Key Principles of the Atlantic Charter\n\n1. No territorial changes against the wishes of the people\n2. The right of peoples to **self-determination** (to choose their own form of government)\n3. Equal access to trade and raw materials\n4. Freedom from want and fear\n5. Freedom of the seas\n6. Disarmament of aggressor nations\n\n### African Responses\n\nThe Atlantic Charter\'s promise of **self-determination** inspired colonised peoples worldwide:\n\n- **The ANC\u2019s Africans\u2019 Claims in South Africa (1943)**: A document drafted by the ANC (largely by **Z.K. Matthews**) that applied the Atlantic Charter\'s principles to South Africa, demanding full citizenship, land reform, and an end to racial discrimination\n- Churchill later clarified that he meant self-determination for European peoples, not colonial subjects \u2014 a betrayal that radicalised many African nationalists\n- The Atlantic Charter influenced the founding of the **United Nations (1945)** and the **Universal Declaration of Human Rights (1948)**'),
  q(6, 'How did the ANC respond to the Atlantic Charter of 1941?',
    ['By drafting "Africans\' Claims in South Africa" (1943), applying the Charter\'s principles of self-determination to South Africa', 'By sending troops to fight in World War II', 'By declaring war on Germany', 'By requesting membership in the United Nations'], 0,
    'The ANC saw the Atlantic Charter\'s promise of self-determination as applying to colonised Africans as well as occupied Europeans. Their response document, Africans\' Claims in South Africa (1943), demanded full citizenship, land reform, and equal rights \u2014 directly challenging segregation.'),
  t(7, '### The ANC Youth League and the Turn to Mass Action\n\nIn **1944**, a group of young intellectuals formed the **ANC Youth League (ANCYL)**, frustrated with the older leadership\u2019s moderate approach:\n\n- **Anton Lembede**: First president of the ANCYL; passionate African nationalist\n- **Nelson Mandela**: Law student; future president of South Africa\n- **Walter Sisulu**: Activist and organiser\n- **Oliver Tambo**: Lawyer and future ANC president in exile\n\nThe Youth League pushed for **mass action** \u2014 strikes, boycotts, and civil disobedience \u2014 instead of polite petitions.\n\nIn **1949**, the Youth League\u2019s **Programme of Action** was adopted by the ANC, committing the organisation to:\n- Boycotts, strikes, and civil disobedience\n- Non-collaboration with oppressive institutions\n- African self-determination\n\nThis marked a fundamental shift in the ANC\u2019s strategy.'),
  t(8, '## The Freedom Charter (26 June 1955)\n\nThe **Freedom Charter** is one of the most important documents in South African history. It was adopted at the **Congress of the People** in Kliptown, Soweto.\n\n### How Was It Created?\n\n- The **Congress Alliance** (ANC, South African Indian Congress, South African Coloured People\u2019s Organisation, Congress of Democrats, South African Congress of Trade Unions) organised the event\n- Volunteers collected demands from ordinary people across South Africa: "If you could have any law, what would it be?"\n- Approximately **3,000 delegates** attended the Congress of the People on 25-26 June 1955\n- On the second day, police arrived and took the names of all delegates\n\n### Key Demands of the Freedom Charter\n\n1. **"The People Shall Govern"** \u2014 universal suffrage (the right to vote for all)\n2. **"All National Groups Shall Have Equal Rights"** \u2014 non-racialism\n3. **"The People Shall Share in the Country\u2019s Wealth"** \u2014 nationalisation of mines, banks, and monopoly industries\n4. **"The Land Shall Be Shared Among Those Who Work It"** \u2014 land reform\n5. **"All Shall Be Equal Before the Law"** \u2014 end to discriminatory laws\n6. **"All Shall Enjoy Equal Human Rights"** \u2014 freedom of speech, movement, and assembly\n7. **"There Shall Be Work and Security"** \u2014 workers\u2019 rights, living wages\n8. **"The Doors of Learning and Culture Shall Be Opened"** \u2014 equal education for all\n9. **"There Shall Be Houses, Security and Comfort"** \u2014 decent housing, healthcare\n10. **"There Shall Be Peace and Friendship"** \u2014 international cooperation'),
  q(9, 'Where was the Freedom Charter adopted in June 1955?',
    ['At the Congress of the People in Kliptown, Soweto', 'At the Union Buildings in Pretoria', 'At the Bloemfontein City Hall', 'At Robben Island'], 0,
    'The Congress of the People was held in Kliptown, Soweto, on 25-26 June 1955. Approximately 3,000 delegates from across South Africa adopted the Freedom Charter, which became the foundational document of the anti-apartheid movement.'),
  fb(10, 'The Freedom Charter was adopted at the Congress of the ___ in 1955. Its first demand was "The People Shall ___."',
    ['People', 'Govern'],
    'The Congress of the People brought together delegates from all racial groups to adopt the Freedom Charter. Its opening demand, "The People Shall Govern," called for universal suffrage \u2014 the right of all South Africans to vote, regardless of race.'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: Afrikaner Nationalism and Middle East/Ghana Case Studies ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch4_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## Afrikaner Nationalism\n\nAfrikaner nationalism was a political movement that sought to promote and protect the interests, language, and culture of **Afrikaners** (white South Africans of Dutch, German, and French Huguenot descent).\n\n### Historical Roots\n\n- **The Great Trek (1830s-1840s)**: Boers (farmers) migrated from the Cape Colony to escape British control; this became a founding myth of Afrikaner identity\n- **The Anglo-Boer Wars (1880-1881, 1899-1902)**: British concentration camps during the Second Boer War killed approximately **26,000 Afrikaner women and children** \u2014 creating deep bitterness towards the British\n- **Union of South Africa (1910)**: Afrikaners were part of the new state but felt culturally and economically dominated by English-speaking white South Africans\n\n### The Broederbond\n\nThe **Afrikaner Broederbond** ("Band of Brothers") was a secret society founded in **1918**:\n- Exclusively white, male, Afrikaner, and Protestant\n- Worked behind the scenes to advance Afrikaner interests in business, education, and politics\n- By the 1940s, members held key positions in government, universities, churches, and the military\n- The Broederbond was instrumental in bringing the National Party to power in 1948'),
  q(2, 'What was the Afrikaner Broederbond?',
    ['A secret society that worked to advance Afrikaner interests in all sectors of South African life', 'An Afrikaner military organisation that fought in World War II', 'A public cultural festival celebrating Afrikaner heritage', 'A political party that competed in elections'], 0,
    'The Broederbond was a secret society founded in 1918 that placed its members in key positions across South African society. It operated behind the scenes to coordinate Afrikaner economic, cultural, and political advancement, playing a crucial role in the rise of the National Party.'),
  t(3, '### The Language Movement and Cultural Organisations\n\n**The Afrikaans Language Movement:**\n- Afrikaans was recognised as an official language in 1925 (alongside English)\n- Language was central to Afrikaner identity: the struggle to have Afrikaans recognised in education, government, and culture was a key nationalist cause\n- The **Tweede Taalbeweging** (Second Language Movement) pushed for Afrikaans to replace Dutch\n\n**The Federasie van Afrikaanse Kultuurvereniginge (FAK):**\n- Founded in **1929** to coordinate Afrikaner cultural organisations\n- Promoted Afrikaans literature, music, theatre, and history\n- Organised cultural festivals and monuments (e.g., the Voortrekker Monument, inaugurated 1949)\n- Worked closely with the Broederbond\n\n**The Economic Programme:**\n- The **Ekonomiese Volkskongres (1939)**: A conference to address Afrikaner poverty and economic weakness\n- Led to the creation of Afrikaner businesses, banks, and insurance companies (e.g., Volkskas, Sanlam, Rembrandt)\n- The **Reddingsdaadbond** ("Rescue Action Society") channelled Afrikaner savings into Afrikaner-owned businesses\n- By the 1970s, Afrikaners had significantly closed the economic gap with English-speaking whites'),
  fb(4, 'The ___ was a cultural organisation founded in 1929 to promote Afrikaans language and culture. The ___ of 1939 was a conference that launched the Afrikaner economic empowerment programme.',
    ['FAK', 'Ekonomiese Volkskongres'],
    'The FAK (Federasie van Afrikaanse Kultuurvereniginge) coordinated Afrikaner cultural life. The Ekonomiese Volkskongres (1939) was a turning point in Afrikaner economic mobilisation, leading to the creation of Afrikaner-owned financial institutions and businesses.'),
  t(5, '## Case Study: The Middle East \u2014 Zionism, Balfour Declaration, and Arab-Israeli Conflict\n\n### Origins of Zionism\n\n**Zionism** is the nationalist movement that sought to establish a Jewish homeland in Palestine:\n- Founded by **Theodor Herzl** after the Dreyfus Affair in France (1894) exposed deep anti-Semitism in Europe\n- Herzl published **Der Judenstaat** ("The Jewish State") in 1896\n- Jews had historical and religious ties to Palestine (the ancient Land of Israel)\n- The Zionist movement organised Jewish immigration to Palestine from the 1880s (the **Aliyah** waves)\n\n### The Balfour Declaration (2 November 1917)\n\nDuring World War I, British Foreign Secretary **Arthur Balfour** wrote a letter to Lord Rothschild stating:\n*"His Majesty\'s Government view with favour the establishment in Palestine of a national home for the Jewish people..."*\n\n**Why did Britain issue it?**\n- To gain Jewish support for the Allied war effort\n- Strategic interests in the Middle East (Suez Canal, oil)\n- Genuine sympathy for Zionism among some British politicians\n\n**The problem:** Britain had also made promises to the Arabs (through the **Hussein-McMahon Correspondence**, 1915-1916) implying Arab independence in the region. These contradictory promises laid the foundation for decades of conflict.'),
  q(6, 'What was the central contradiction in British policy that contributed to the Arab-Israeli conflict?',
    ['Britain promised both a Jewish homeland (Balfour Declaration) and Arab independence (Hussein-McMahon Correspondence) in the same territory', 'Britain promised to stay neutral in the Middle East', 'Britain supported only the Arab side in the conflict', 'Britain refused to get involved in Middle Eastern affairs'], 0,
    'Britain made contradictory promises during WWI: the Balfour Declaration (1917) supported a Jewish homeland in Palestine, while the Hussein-McMahon Correspondence (1915-1916) implied Arab independence in the same region. This double-dealing was a root cause of the Arab-Israeli conflict.'),
  t(7, '### The Arab-Israeli Conflict\n\n**The British Mandate (1920-1948):**\n- After WWI, Britain controlled Palestine under a League of Nations Mandate\n- Jewish immigration increased, especially after the rise of Nazism (1933)\n- Arab Palestinians resisted Jewish immigration and land purchases\n- **The Arab Revolt (1936-1939)**: Palestinian Arabs revolted against British rule and Jewish immigration\n- Britain tried (and failed) to balance Jewish and Arab demands\n\n**The Creation of Israel (1948):**\n- After the Holocaust, international support for a Jewish state surged\n- In **November 1947**, the UN voted to partition Palestine into Jewish and Arab states\n- On **14 May 1948**, David Ben-Gurion declared the independence of **Israel**\n- Immediately, neighbouring Arab states (Egypt, Jordan, Syria, Iraq, Lebanon) invaded\n- Israel won the **1948 War of Independence / Nakba ("Catastrophe")**: approximately **700,000 Palestinian Arabs** fled or were expelled from their homes\n\nThe Palestinian refugee crisis created by the Nakba remains unresolved to this day. The conflict between Israeli nationalism (Zionism) and Palestinian nationalism continues to be one of the world\u2019s most intractable disputes.'),
  fb(8, 'The Balfour ___ of 1917 promised a Jewish homeland in Palestine. The Palestinian displacement of 1948, when approximately 700,000 Arabs fled or were expelled, is known as the ___.',
    ['Declaration', 'Nakba'],
    'The Balfour Declaration (1917) was the first international recognition of the Zionist goal. The Nakba ("Catastrophe") of 1948 refers to the mass displacement of Palestinian Arabs during the creation of Israel \u2014 a traumatic event that remains central to Palestinian identity.'),
  t(9, '## Case Study: Ghana \u2014 From Gold Coast to Independence\n\n### Colonial Background\n\n- The **Gold Coast** (modern Ghana) was a British colony rich in cocoa, gold, and timber\n- Colonialism brought some infrastructure (railways, schools, hospitals) but primarily served British economic interests\n- An educated African elite emerged, frustrated by racial discrimination and colonial rule\n\n### Kwame Nkrumah and the Independence Movement\n\n- **Kwame Nkrumah** studied in the USA and Britain, where he was influenced by **Pan-Africanism** (the belief that all African peoples share common interests and should unite)\n- In 1947, he returned to the Gold Coast and joined the United Gold Coast Convention (UGCC)\n- In 1949, he broke away to form the **Convention People\u2019s Party (CPP)**, with the slogan **"Self-Government Now!"**\n- The CPP used mass action: strikes, boycotts, and civil disobedience ("Positive Action")\n- Nkrumah was imprisoned by the British but the CPP won the 1951 elections overwhelmingly\n- He was released from prison to become Leader of Government Business, then Prime Minister\n\n### Independence (6 March 1957)\n\n- Ghana became the **first sub-Saharan African country** to gain independence from colonial rule\n- Nkrumah declared: *"We face neither East nor West; we face forward"*\n- Ghana\u2019s independence inspired nationalist movements across Africa'),
  q(10, 'Why was Ghana\'s independence in 1957 historically significant for the rest of Africa?',
    ['It was the first sub-Saharan African country to gain independence, inspiring nationalist movements across the continent', 'It was the wealthiest African country', 'It was the only African country to gain independence peacefully', 'It immediately became a global superpower'], 0,
    'Ghana\u2019s independence on 6 March 1957 was a watershed moment for Africa. It proved that African self-governance was possible and directly inspired independence movements in Nigeria, Kenya, Tanzania, and many other colonies. Nkrumah became a symbol of African liberation.'),
  t(11, '### Pan-Africanism\n\n**Pan-Africanism** is the belief that people of African descent worldwide share common interests and should work together for liberation and unity.\n\n**Key figures:**\n- **W.E.B. Du Bois** (USA): Organised early Pan-African Congresses (1919, 1921, 1923, 1927)\n- **Marcus Garvey** (Jamaica): Founded the Universal Negro Improvement Association; promoted "Back to Africa" movement\n- **Kwame Nkrumah** (Ghana): Called for political unity of African states; helped found the Organisation of African Unity (OAU) in 1963\n\n**The Fifth Pan-African Congress (Manchester, 1945):**\n- Attended by future African leaders including Nkrumah and Jomo Kenyatta (Kenya)\n- Demanded immediate independence for all African colonies\n- Marked a shift from gradual reform to militant anti-colonialism\n\n**Relevance to South Africa:**\n- The ANC was influenced by Pan-Africanist ideas\n- The **Pan Africanist Congress (PAC)** broke from the ANC in 1959 under **Robert Sobukwe**, arguing for a more Africanist (rather than multi-racial) approach\n- Pan-Africanism challenged the idea that Africa was "not ready" for self-governance \u2014 an argument used by both colonial and apartheid regimes'),
  q(12, 'Which event in 1945 marked a turning point in the Pan-African movement by demanding immediate independence for all African colonies?',
    ['The Fifth Pan-African Congress in Manchester', 'The founding of the United Nations', 'The end of World War II', 'The Atlantic Charter conference'], 0,
    'The Fifth Pan-African Congress in Manchester (1945) was a turning point because it shifted from requesting gradual reform to demanding immediate independence. Future leaders like Nkrumah and Kenyatta attended, and they returned home to lead independence movements.'),
];

// =============================================================================
