// --- Lesson 1: Segregation, the NP Victory, and the Creation of the Apartheid State ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch5_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## How Unique Was Apartheid?\n\nApartheid ("apartness" in Afrikaans) was the system of racial segregation and discrimination enforced by the South African government from **1948 to 1994**. While racial discrimination existed in many countries, apartheid was **unique** in several ways:\n\n1. **It was legally codified**: Racial discrimination was written into hundreds of laws, not just practised informally\n2. **It was comprehensive**: Every aspect of life \u2014 where you lived, worked, went to school, could marry, could travel \u2014 was determined by race\n3. **It was implemented AFTER the Holocaust**: The world had just witnessed where racial ideology could lead, yet South Africa chose to build a racial state\n4. **It was imposed by a minority on a majority**: White South Africans (less than 20% of the population) ruled over the black majority\n5. **It lasted for over 40 years**: Unlike temporary wartime measures, apartheid was intended to be permanent\n\nHowever, apartheid did not appear from nowhere. It built on decades of **segregation** that preceded it.'),
  q(2, 'What made apartheid unique compared to racial discrimination in other countries?',
    ['It was legally codified, comprehensive, imposed by a minority on a majority, and implemented after the Holocaust', 'It was the first time any country practised racial discrimination', 'It was only practised in rural areas', 'It lasted less than ten years'], 0,
    'Apartheid was distinguished by its comprehensive legal framework, the fact that a racial minority imposed it on the majority, and the troubling timing \u2014 it was created just three years after the Holocaust showed the world the extreme consequences of racial ideology.'),
  t(3, '## Segregation in the 1920s-1930s: The Foundations of Apartheid\n\nRacial segregation in South Africa did not begin with apartheid in 1948. Key segregation legislation before 1948 included:\n\n| Law | Year | Purpose |\n|-----|------|----------|\n| **Mines and Works Act** | 1911 | Reserved skilled mining jobs for whites (colour bar) |\n| **Natives Land Act** | 1913 | Restricted black land ownership to 7.3% of South Africa |\n| **Native (Urban Areas) Act** | 1923 | Controlled black urbanisation; pass laws; separate townships |\n| **Industrial Conciliation Act** | 1924 | Excluded black workers from registered trade unions |\n| **Mines and Works Amendment Act (Colour Bar Act)** | 1926 | Extended job reservation for whites in mining and industry |\n| **Native Administration Act** | 1927 | Gave government control over all "native affairs" |\n| **Native Trust and Land Act** | 1936 | Increased reserves to 13% of land; removed Cape African voters from common roll |\n| **Representation of Natives Act** | 1936 | Removed Cape African voters from common voters\' roll |\n\n### Hertzog\'s "Segregation Bills" (1936)\n\nPrime Minister **J.B.M. Hertzog** passed laws in 1936 that removed the limited voting rights that some black men had in the Cape Province. This was a significant step towards complete political exclusion.'),
  fb(4, 'The Natives ___ Act of 1913 restricted black land ownership to 7.3% of South Africa. In 1936, Prime Minister ___ removed Cape African voters from the common voters\' roll.',
    ['Land', 'Hertzog'],
    'The 1913 Natives Land Act was the foundation of territorial segregation. Hertzog\'s "Segregation Bills" of 1936 completed the political exclusion of black South Africans from the common voters\' roll, laying the groundwork for apartheid.'),
  t(5, '## The National Party Victory of 1948\n\nIn the **1948 general election**, the **National Party (NP)** under **D.F. Malan** won an upset victory against Jan Smuts\'s United Party.\n\n### Why Did the NP Win?\n\n1. **The "swart gevaar" ("black danger") campaign**: The NP exploited white fears of black urbanisation and competition for jobs\n2. **Afrikaner nationalism**: The NP appealed to Afrikaner identity, language, and culture\n3. **Support from the Broederbond**: The secret society mobilised Afrikaner voters and resources\n4. **Rural over-representation**: Electoral boundaries favoured rural areas where the NP was strong\n5. **Opposition division**: Smuts\'s United Party was seen as too English, too moderate, and out of touch\n6. **Post-war insecurity**: White workers feared economic competition from black workers who had gained skills during WWII\n\n### The NP\'s Apartheid Platform\n\nThe NP explicitly campaigned on a platform of **apartheid** (racial separation). They promised:\n- Strict separation of races\n- Preservation of white supremacy\n- Protection of white jobs\n- Separate development for each racial group'),
  q(6, 'What was the "swart gevaar" strategy used by the National Party in the 1948 election?',
    ['Exploiting white fears of the "black danger" of African urbanisation and job competition', 'A plan to develop rural black areas', 'A military strategy against neighbouring countries', 'A policy to encourage immigration from Europe'], 0,
    'The "swart gevaar" (Afrikaans for "black danger") was a fear-mongering tactic used by the NP. They portrayed growing black urbanisation and economic advancement as a threat to white jobs, safety, and culture. This racial fear was central to their 1948 election victory.'),
  t(7, '## The Creation of the Apartheid State: Key Laws\n\nAfter 1948, the NP passed a series of laws that created the legal framework of apartheid:\n\n### Laws of Classification and Separation\n\n| Law | Year | Purpose |\n|-----|------|---------|\n| **Prohibition of Mixed Marriages Act** | 1949 | Banned marriages between white people and people of other races |\n| **Immorality Amendment Act** | 1950 | Banned sexual relations between whites and other races |\n| **Population Registration Act** | 1950 | Classified every South African by race (White, Coloured, Indian, Native/Bantu) |\n| **Group Areas Act** | 1950 | Designated specific areas for each racial group; forced removals of "wrong" race |\n| **Suppression of Communism Act** | 1950 | Banned the CPSA; defined "communism" so broadly it could target any opposition |\n| **Bantu Authorities Act** | 1951 | Created a system of tribal authorities in the reserves, undermining traditional leadership |\n| **Natives (Abolition of Passes) Act** | 1952 | Despite its name, actually EXTENDED pass laws to all black men and women |\n| **Separate Amenities Act** | 1953 | Segregated public facilities (buses, beaches, benches, toilets, etc.) with no requirement for equality |\n| **Bantu Education Act** | 1953 | Created an inferior education system for black children designed to prepare them for manual labour |'),
  t(8, '### Hendrik Verwoerd and "Grand Apartheid"\n\n**Hendrik Verwoerd**, Minister of Native Affairs (1950-1958) and Prime Minister (1958-1966), was the chief architect of apartheid.\n\nVerwoerd developed the concept of **"Grand Apartheid"** or **"Separate Development"**:\n- Each racial group would develop "separately" in their own area\n- Black South Africans would exercise political rights in **Bantustans (homelands)** based on ethnic group\n- The Bantu Self-Government Act (1959) established eight (later ten) Bantustans\n- The goal was to make every black South African a "citizen" of a Bantustan, effectively denying them South African citizenship\n\n**The reality of the Bantustans:**\n- They comprised only **13%** of South Africa\u2019s land for over 70% of the population\n- They were in the poorest, most remote areas with little infrastructure\n- Their economies were not viable; they depended on migrant labour to white areas\n- Their "independence" was a fiction \u2014 no other country in the world recognised them\n- Four Bantustans were declared "independent": Transkei (1976), Bophuthatswana (1977), Venda (1979), Ciskei (1981)'),
  q(9, 'What was the real purpose of the Bantustan (homeland) system?',
    ['To deny black South Africans citizenship and political rights in South Africa by making them "citizens" of impoverished homelands', 'To provide genuine self-governance for black communities', 'To create wealthy, independent black states', 'To allow black South Africans to vote in national elections'], 0,
    'The Bantustan system was designed to strip black South Africans of their citizenship in South Africa. By declaring them citizens of "independent" homelands (on just 13% of the land), the apartheid government could claim that there was no black majority in "white" South Africa.'),
  fb(10, 'The ___ Registration Act of 1950 classified every South African by race. Hendrik ___ was the chief architect of "Grand Apartheid" and the Bantustan system.',
    ['Population', 'Verwoerd'],
    'The Population Registration Act was the cornerstone of apartheid \u2014 without racial classification, the entire system would collapse. Verwoerd designed the Bantustan system as the ideological framework for permanently denying black political rights in "white" South Africa.'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: Resistance to Apartheid ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch5_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## Laws Against Multiracial Labour and Coloured Resistance\n\n### Labour Laws Under Apartheid\n\nThe apartheid government used labour legislation to protect white economic privilege:\n\n- **Native Labour (Settlement of Disputes) Act (1953)**: Made strikes by black workers illegal\n- **Industrial Conciliation Act (1956)**: Extended job reservation; banned multiracial trade unions\n- **Mines and Works Act amendments**: Reserved skilled jobs for whites in mining\n- **Bantu Labour Act (1964)**: Regulated black labour; forced workers into labour bureaux\n\nThese laws ensured that black workers remained a cheap, controlled labour force serving white-owned industries.\n\n### Coloured Resistance: The Franchise Fight\n\nColoured South Africans (people of mixed heritage) in the Cape Province had limited voting rights on the common voters\' roll. The NP was determined to remove this:\n\n- **The Separate Representation of Voters Act (1951)**: Attempted to remove Coloured voters from the common roll\n- **The Constitutional Crisis (1951-1956)**: The courts ruled the Act unconstitutional; the NP increased the Senate to get the two-thirds majority needed\n- **The Torch Commando**: A protest organisation of white war veterans opposed to the removal of Coloured voting rights\n- By **1956**, the NP had successfully removed Coloured voters from the common roll\n\nThis was a reminder that apartheid did not target only black South Africans \u2014 Coloured and Indian communities were also systematically stripped of rights.'),
  q(2, 'How did the NP overcome the constitutional obstacle to removing Coloured voters from the common roll?',
    ['By increasing the size of the Senate to get the required two-thirds majority', 'By holding a national referendum', 'By asking the British Parliament for permission', 'By declaring a state of emergency'], 0,
    'The NP packed the Senate with its supporters to achieve the two-thirds majority required to amend the constitution. This manipulation of the constitution to achieve a political goal showed that the NP would bend or break any rule to implement apartheid.'),
  t(3, '## Black Resistance: The Defiance Campaign and Beyond\n\n### The Defiance Campaign (1952)\n\nThe **Defiance Campaign** was the first large-scale, multiracial resistance to apartheid:\n\n- Launched on **26 June 1952** (now celebrated as Freedom Day in SA)\n- Organised by the ANC and the South African Indian Congress\n- Volunteers deliberately broke apartheid laws: using "whites only" facilities, entering locations without permits, breaking curfew\n- Over **8,500 volunteers were arrested** across the country\n- ANC membership surged from 7,000 to 100,000\n\n**Impact:**\n- Demonstrated that ordinary people were willing to go to jail for their rights\n- Built the Congress Alliance (ANC, SAIC, SACPO, COD, SACTU)\n- The government responded with harsher laws, including the Criminal Law Amendment Act (1953) and the Public Safety Act (1953)\n\n### The Women\'s March (9 August 1956)\n\nIn **1956**, the government extended pass laws to women. On **9 August**, approximately **20,000 women** of all races marched to the Union Buildings in Pretoria to deliver a petition to Prime Minister Strijdom.\n\nLed by **Lillian Ngoyi, Helen Joseph, Rahima Moosa, and Sophie de Bruyn**, the women sang: *"Wathint\u2019 abafazi, wathint\u2019 imbokodo, uzokufa"* ("You strike the women, you strike a rock, you will be crushed"). 9 August is now **National Women\u2019s Day** in South Africa.'),
  fb(4, 'The ___ Campaign of 1952 was the first large-scale resistance to apartheid, with over 8,500 volunteers arrested. The Women\'s March of 9 August ___ saw 20,000 women protest pass laws at the Union Buildings.',
    ['Defiance', '1956'],
    'The Defiance Campaign (1952) was inspired by Gandhian principles of non-violent civil disobedience. The 1956 Women\'s March was one of the largest demonstrations in South African history, showing that women were at the forefront of the anti-apartheid struggle.'),
  t(5, '### The Treason Trial (1956-1961)\n\nIn December 1956, the government arrested **156 leaders** of the Congress Alliance and charged them with high treason, alleging a communist-inspired conspiracy to overthrow the state.\n\n- The trial lasted **five years** (1956-1961), the longest in South African legal history\n- Defendants included Nelson Mandela, Walter Sisulu, Chief Albert Luthuli, and others\n- The defence argued that the Freedom Charter was a democratic document, not a communist one\n- All defendants were eventually **acquitted** (found not guilty) in March 1961\n- The trial was a pyrrhic victory for the government: it tied up opposition leaders but also gave them a national platform and international attention\n\n### The Banning of the CPSA and Political Organisations\n\nThe **Suppression of Communism Act (1950)** banned the Communist Party of South Africa (CPSA). Its definition of "communism" was deliberately vague and could include:\n- Any doctrine that aimed to bring about political change\n- Any organisation that sought to advance the interests of "any particular class"\n\nThis meant the government could (and did) label virtually any opposition organisation or individual as "communist." Hundreds of activists were **banned** (prohibited from attending meetings, publishing, or being quoted) under this Act.'),
  q(6, 'What was the outcome of the Treason Trial (1956-1961)?',
    ['All 156 defendants were acquitted after a five-year trial', 'All defendants were found guilty and sentenced to life imprisonment', 'The trial was abandoned after one year', 'Only Nelson Mandela was found guilty'], 0,
    'All defendants in the Treason Trial were acquitted in March 1961. The judge found that the Freedom Charter was not a communist document and that the prosecution had failed to prove a conspiracy to overthrow the state by violence. The trial lasted five years and was a major embarrassment for the government.'),
  t(7, '### The Sharpeville Massacre (21 March 1960)\n\nThe **Pan Africanist Congress (PAC)**, which had broken from the ANC in 1959 under **Robert Sobukwe**, launched an anti-pass campaign on 21 March 1960.\n\nAt **Sharpeville**, near Vereeniging:\n- A crowd of approximately **5,000-7,000 people** gathered at the police station to surrender their passes\n- Police opened fire on the crowd without warning\n- **69 people were killed** and over **180 wounded**, many shot in the back as they fled\n\n**Consequences:**\n- International outrage and condemnation\n- The United Nations passed its first resolution against South Africa\n- A **State of Emergency** was declared\n- Both the **ANC and PAC were banned** (April 1960)\n- The ANC and PAC went underground and established **armed wings**: the ANC formed **Umkhonto we Sizwe (MK)** and the PAC formed **Poqo**\n- Nelson Mandela went underground; MK launched its first sabotage attacks on **16 December 1961**\n\nSharpeville marked the end of peaceful protest and the beginning of armed resistance. 21 March is now **Human Rights Day** in South Africa.'),
  fb(8, 'On 21 March 1960, police shot and killed ___ people at Sharpeville during a peaceful anti-pass protest. After both the ANC and PAC were banned, the ANC formed the armed wing called ___ we Sizwe (MK).',
    ['69', 'Umkhonto'],
    'The Sharpeville Massacre was a turning point in South African history. The killing of 69 peaceful protesters convinced many that non-violent methods alone could not defeat apartheid. Umkhonto we Sizwe ("Spear of the Nation") carried out sabotage attacks against government infrastructure.'),
  t(9, '## Overcoming Resistance to Apartheid: State Repression\n\nThe apartheid government used increasingly brutal methods to suppress opposition:\n\n### Security Legislation\n\n- **Sabotage Act (1962)**: Death penalty for sabotage; broad definition\n- **General Law Amendment Act (1963, "90-day detention")**: Allowed detention without trial for 90 days; later extended to 180 days, then indefinite\n- **Terrorism Act (1967)**: Extremely broad definition of "terrorism"; allowed indefinite detention\n\n### The Rivonia Trial (1963-1964)\n\n- In July 1963, police raided **Liliesleaf Farm** in Rivonia, arresting MK leaders including Walter Sisulu, Govan Mbeki, Ahmed Kathrada, and others\n- Nelson Mandela (already imprisoned) was added to the trial\n- Charged with sabotage and conspiracy to overthrow the state\n- Mandela\u2019s speech from the dock: *"I have cherished the ideal of a democratic and free society... It is an ideal which I hope to live for and to achieve. But if needs be, it is an ideal for which I am prepared to die."*\n- **Eight defendants were sentenced to life imprisonment** on Robben Island (June 1964)\n\nThe Rivonia Trial removed the ANC\u2019s top leadership for decades. The 1960s became known as the "silent decade" in the anti-apartheid struggle, though resistance continued underground and in exile.'),
  q(10, 'Why is Nelson Mandela\'s speech from the dock at the Rivonia Trial considered historically significant?',
    ['He expressed his willingness to die for the ideal of a democratic and free society, inspiring generations of resistance', 'He confessed to all charges against him', 'He called for immediate violent revolution', 'He negotiated a deal with the government'], 0,
    'Mandela\'s speech from the dock became one of the most famous statements of political principle in the 20th century. By saying he was "prepared to die" for a free and democratic society, he transformed the trial from a defeat into a moral victory that inspired the anti-apartheid struggle for decades.'),
];

