blockNum = 0;
const ch6_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## Grade 11 History Exam Structure\n\nThe Grade 11 History examination consists of two papers:\n\n### Paper 1: 150 marks (3 hours)\n\nCovers **Term 1 and early Term 2** content:\n- **Section A**: Source-based questions\n- **Section B**: Essay questions\n\n| Topic | Content |\n|-------|---------|\n| **Communism in Russia 1900-1940** | Marx, 1917 revolutions, Lenin, NEP, Stalin (collectivisation, industrialisation, purges, women) |\n| **Capitalism in the USA 1900-1940** | American Dream, 1920s boom, Wall Street Crash, Great Depression, FDR and the New Deal |\n| **Ideas of Race** | Race hierarchies, eugenics, Human Genome Project, case studies (Australia/Holocaust) |\n\n### Paper 2: 150 marks (3 hours)\n\nCovers **Term 2 and Term 3** content:\n- **Section A**: Source-based questions\n- **Section B**: Essay questions\n\n| Topic | Content |\n|-------|---------|\n| **Nationalisms** | African nationalism (SANNC/ANC, Freedom Charter), Afrikaner nationalism (Broederbond, FAK), Middle East (Zionism), Ghana |\n| **Apartheid South Africa** | Segregation, NP victory 1948, apartheid laws, resistance (Defiance Campaign, Sharpeville, Rivonia Trial) |'),
  q(2, 'How many marks is each paper in the Grade 11 History exam?',
    ['150 marks', '100 marks', '200 marks', '75 marks'], 0,
    'Each paper is worth 150 marks and lasts 3 hours. Paper 1 covers Communism, Capitalism, and Ideas of Race. Paper 2 covers Nationalisms and Apartheid South Africa. Together they test both source-based skills and essay writing.'),
  t(3, '## Source-Based Question Technique\n\nSource-based questions test your ability to analyse and interpret historical evidence. Here is a step-by-step approach:\n\n### Types of Sources\n\n| Type | Examples |\n|------|----------|\n| **Written sources** | Letters, speeches, newspaper articles, government documents, memoirs |\n| **Visual sources** | Photographs, cartoons, posters, paintings, maps |\n| **Statistical sources** | Tables, graphs, charts |\n\n### Key Question Types and How to Answer Them\n\n**1. Extraction (Level 1)** \u2014 "According to the source..."\n- Quote directly from the source\n- Use quotation marks\n- Keep it brief and relevant\n\n**2. Explanation (Level 2)** \u2014 "Explain what the source means by..."\n- Use your own words AND the source\n- Add context from your own knowledge\n- Link to the historical period\n\n**3. Comparison (Level 3)** \u2014 "Compare Source A and Source B..."\n- Find similarities AND differences\n- Quote from BOTH sources\n- Structure: "Source A says... while Source B says..."\n\n**4. Evaluation (Level 3)** \u2014 "How useful/reliable is this source?"\n- Consider: Who wrote it? When? Why? For what audience?\n- Identify bias, perspective, and limitations\n- Compare with your own knowledge\n- Conclusion: Is it useful/reliable? To what extent?'),
  fb(4, 'When comparing two sources, you must find both ___ and differences between them. When evaluating reliability, you should consider who wrote it, when, why, and for what ___.',
    ['similarities', 'audience'],
    'Comparison questions require you to identify both similarities and differences, quoting from both sources. Evaluation requires analysing the origin, purpose, and context of a source to assess its usefulness and reliability.'),
  t(5, '## Essay Writing: The PEEL Method\n\nHistory essays require a structured argument. Use the **PEEL method** for each paragraph:\n\n### PEEL Structure\n\n| Letter | Meaning | What to Do |\n|--------|---------|------------|\n| **P** | Point | State the main argument of the paragraph clearly |\n| **E** | Evidence | Provide specific historical facts, dates, names, and events |\n| **E** | Explanation | Explain HOW the evidence supports your point |\n| **L** | Link | Link back to the essay question/thesis |\n\n### Essay Structure\n\n**Introduction (about 5 marks)**\n1. Define key terms from the question\n2. Provide brief historical context\n3. State your **thesis** (main argument / line of argument)\n\n**Body Paragraphs (about 30-35 marks)**\n- 4-6 PEEL paragraphs\n- Each paragraph = one main point with evidence\n- Use **topic sentences** to start each paragraph\n- Include **dates, names, and specific details**\n\n**Conclusion (about 5 marks)**\n1. Summarise your main points\n2. Restate your thesis in light of the evidence\n3. Make a final judgement / take a position\n\n### Time Management\n\nWith 150 marks in 3 hours:\n- You have approximately **1 minute per mark**\n- Source questions: aim for 45-50 minutes per question\n- Essays: aim for 45-50 minutes per essay (including 5 minutes planning)'),
  q(6, 'In the PEEL essay method, what does the first E stand for?',
    ['Evidence \u2014 providing specific historical facts, dates, and events', 'Explanation \u2014 explaining how evidence supports your point', 'Evaluation \u2014 assessing the reliability of sources', 'Example \u2014 giving a general example of the topic'], 0,
    'In PEEL, the first E stands for Evidence. After stating your Point, you must back it up with specific historical evidence: dates, names, events, statistics, or quotations. The second E (Explanation) then explains how that evidence supports your argument.'),
  t(7, '## Key Content Review: Paper 1 Topics\n\n### Communism in Russia \u2014 Key Points to Remember\n\n- Marx: class struggle, Communist Manifesto (1848), bourgeoisie vs proletariat\n- Russia before revolution: Tsar Nicholas II, autocracy, poverty, opposition groups\n- February 1917: spontaneous uprising, abdication of Tsar, Provisional Government and soviets\n- October 1917: Lenin, April Theses ("Peace, Land, Bread"), Bolshevik coup organised by Trotsky\n- Consolidation: Brest-Litovsk, Constituent Assembly dissolved, Cheka, Civil War (1918-1921)\n- NEP (1921-1928): tactical retreat, Kronstadt mutiny, limited capitalism\n- Power struggle: Lenin dies 1924, Stalin vs Trotsky, "socialism in one country"\n- Stalin: collectivisation (kulaks, Holodomor), Five-Year Plans, purges (show trials, Gulag), women\n\n### Capitalism in the USA \u2014 Key Points to Remember\n\n- Capitalism: private ownership, free market, profit motive, American Dream\n- 1920s boom: mass production (Ford), consumer goods, buying on credit/margin, uneven prosperity\n- Wall Street Crash: Black Thursday (24 Oct 1929), Black Tuesday (29 Oct 1929)\n- Great Depression: 25% unemployment, Hoovervilles, Dust Bowl, bank failures\n- FDR and the New Deal: First Hundred Days, alphabet agencies (CCC, WPA, TVA), Social Security Act\n- Assessment: reduced unemployment but did not end Depression; WWII ended it'),
  t(8, '### Ideas of Race \u2014 Key Points to Remember\n\n- 19th century: Linnaeus, Blumenbach, Social Darwinism, phrenology\n- Eugenics: Francis Galton, positive and negative eugenics, forced sterilisation\n- Case studies: USA (sterilisation, Tuskegee), Australia (Stolen Generations), Namibia (Herero genocide), SA (racial classification)\n- Nazi Germany: Aryan ideology, Nuremberg Laws, Kristallnacht, Holocaust (6 million Jews), Wannsee Conference, death camps\n- Human Genome Project: 99.9% shared DNA, race is a social construct\n\n## Key Content Review: Paper 2 Topics\n\n### Nationalisms \u2014 Key Points to Remember\n\n- African nationalism: SANNC (1912), 1913 Land Act, ANC Youth League (1944), Programme of Action, Freedom Charter (1955, Kliptown)\n- Atlantic Charter (1941): self-determination, Africans\u2019 Claims (1943)\n- Afrikaner nationalism: Broederbond (1918), FAK (1929), language movement, Ekonomiese Volkskongres (1939)\n- Middle East: Zionism (Herzl), Balfour Declaration (1917), 1948 creation of Israel, Nakba\n- Ghana: Nkrumah, CPP, first sub-Saharan independence (1957), Pan-Africanism\n\n### Apartheid SA \u2014 Key Points to Remember\n\n- Pre-1948 segregation: Land Act, pass laws, colour bar\n- NP victory 1948: swart gevaar, Broederbond support\n- Apartheid laws: Population Registration, Group Areas, Bantu Education, Separate Amenities\n- Grand Apartheid: Verwoerd, Bantustans\n- Resistance: Defiance Campaign (1952), Women\u2019s March (1956), Sharpeville (1960), MK, Rivonia Trial (1964)'),
  q(9, 'When writing a history essay, what should you include in your introduction?',
    ['Definitions of key terms, historical context, and your thesis (line of argument)', 'A detailed description of all the evidence you will use', 'A summary of what other historians have written', 'Only a restatement of the essay question'], 0,
    'A strong introduction should: (1) define key terms from the question, (2) provide brief historical context to set the scene, and (3) state your thesis \u2014 your main argument or line of argument that you will develop throughout the essay.'),
  t(10, '## Exam Tips for Grade 11 History\n\n### General Tips\n\n1. **Read the question carefully**: Underline key words (discuss, explain, compare, evaluate, to what extent)\n2. **Plan before you write**: Spend 5 minutes on a brief essay plan (thesis + main points)\n3. **Use specific evidence**: Dates, names, places, statistics \u2014 not vague generalities\n4. **Answer the question**: Stay focused; do not write everything you know about the topic\n5. **Manage your time**: Do not spend too long on one question at the expense of others\n6. **Use paragraphs**: Each paragraph = one main idea\n7. **Quote sources**: In source-based questions, always reference the specific source\n8. **Take a position**: History rewards those who can make a judgement and support it with evidence\n\n### Common Mistakes to Avoid\n\n- Writing a narrative (story) instead of an argument\n- Ignoring the specific focus of the question\n- Not using evidence from the sources provided\n- Running out of time on the last question\n- Not answering all parts of a multi-part question\n- Confusing dates and events (e.g., mixing up February and October revolutions)\n- Using vague language ("a lot of people were affected") instead of specific facts\n\n### Final Thought\n\nHistory is not just about memorising facts. It is about understanding **why** events happened, **how** they connect to each other, and **what they mean** for us today. The themes of this course \u2014 communism, capitalism, race, nationalism, and apartheid \u2014 are not just historical. They continue to shape the world you live in.'),
  fb(11, 'With 150 marks in 3 hours, you have approximately ___ minute(s) per mark. The introduction of an essay should define key terms, provide context, and state your ___ (line of argument).',
    ['1', 'thesis'],
    'With 150 marks in 3 hours, you have approximately 1 minute per mark. The introduction should define key terms, provide historical context, and state your thesis (argument/line of argument) to set up the essay.'),
];

